  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How should a project lead make operational decisions when choosing between delivery options with incomplete data?$qb$, $qb$Mitigating downside risks by evaluating potential client impact, selecting reliable options, and setting checks.$qb$, $qb$Selecting the fastest theoretical option to maximize speed potential, monitoring progress along the way.$qb$, $qb$Delaying operational choices for several hours to wait for updated background data reports.$qb$, $qb$Asking the client to evaluate risk trade-offs and choose their preferred delivery pathway.$qb$, $qb$Splitting resources equally across available delivery options to hedge operational risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process becomes faster, but errors increase later in the workflow. Which response best addresses the overall problem?$qb$, $qb$Evaluating systemic trade-offs, restoring essential quality checks, and redesigning workflows to balance speed.$qb$, $qb$Adding warning documentation to deliverables advising clients to verify contents upon receipt.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining high processing speeds while establishing dedicated fast-track teams to handle downstream errors.$qb$, $qb$Reverting strictly to original slower workflows to eliminate downstream operational errors completely.$qb$, $qb$Transferring downstream error tracking metrics to support teams to handle post-delivery complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team spends significant time on an old copy-paste reconciliation task. What is the best way to decide how the work should be redesigned?$qb$, $qb$Deconstructing underlying data inputs and reporting needs, eliminating redundant steps, and automating flow.$qb$, $qb$Searching for advanced spreadsheet macros to execute existing copy-paste steps more quickly.$qb$, $qb$Dividing manual copy-paste duties among multiple team members to reduce individual time burden.$qb$, $qb$Recommending that reconciliation task frequencies be reduced from weekly to monthly to save hours.$qb$, $qb$Executing the process exactly as documented to maintain historical tracking continuity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the primary goal when synthesizing a dense multi-page industry report into an executive briefing?$qb$, $qb$Distilling critical strategic signals, removing operational noise, and presenting actionable insights.$qb$, $qb$Copying key executive summary sections and visual data charts directly into a condensed document.$qb$, $qb$Creating a detailed section-by-section outline covering all topics presented in the original report.$qb$, $qb$Presenting raw numerical data tables exclusively, omitting narrative context to keep briefings short.$qb$, $qb$Compiling individual chapter summaries written by department specialists into a unified document.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team claims a new process improved quality because errors fell by half, but the number of completed cases also fell sharply. What should you examine?$qb$, $qb$Compare error rates and relevant volumes before concluding the process improved quality$qb$, $qb$Calculate errors as a proportion of completed cases$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement because the number of errors fell$qb$, $qb$Reject the improvement because output volume fell$qb$, $qb$Use whichever measure supports the team's conclusion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A service issue has been fixed several times but keeps returning in slightly different forms. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Look across the cases for a common underlying cause and redesign the part of the process creating it$qb$, $qb$Compare the recurring cases and identify the common pattern$qb$, $qb$Create a faster standard fix for each new case$qb$, $qb$Assign a specialist to handle all future cases$qb$, $qb$Treat each case separately because the symptoms differ$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two reasonable approaches, but the most important uncertainty will not be resolved for a week. Waiting also has a cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Assess the downside of being wrong, choose a reversible step where possible, and define what evidence would trigger a change$qb$, $qb$Compare the risks of acting now versus waiting and choose the lower-risk path$qb$, $qb$Wait for certainty because the decision matters$qb$, $qb$Choose the approach with the highest possible benefit$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior person to take responsibility for the decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A local improvement reduces processing time but increases customer contacts and support workload. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate the total customer and operational impact, then redesign the change so the overall system improves$qb$, $qb$Measure the extra contacts and work with support to identify the cause$qb$, $qb$Keep the improvement because processing time is the team's target$qb$, $qb$Ask support to automate the extra contacts$qb$, $qb$Reverse the change immediately without measuring the benefits$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A monthly approval report contains many sections copied from older versions, but nobody can explain why all are needed. What is the best starting point?$qb$, $qb$Define the decisions and risks the report must support, then test each section against those needs$qb$, $qb$Ask current readers which sections they actually use$qb$, $qb$Remove the sections that receive little attention$qb$, $qb$Automate the report so the extra sections cost less time$qb$, $qb$Keep the report unchanged because it supports approval$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to advise a manager using data from several sources that partly disagree. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile material differences, distinguish strong evidence from weak signals, and state what remains uncertain$qb$, $qb$Compare the sources, explain the main differences, and identify the most reliable evidence$qb$, $qb$Average the conflicting numbers$qb$, $qb$Use the source with the most detail$qb$, $qb$Present all sources without judging their reliability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A survey result is based on five responses from a team of one hundred. The conclusion could influence a team decision. What is the main concern?$qb$, $qb$The sample is too small to support a strong conclusion about the whole team$qb$, $qb$The five respondents may not represent the full team$qb$, $qb$The survey may contain poorly worded questions$qb$, $qb$The result may still be correct if all five agree$qb$, $qb$There is no concern if the percentage is high$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task is delayed every Monday but usually runs on time later in the week. A quick fix has already been tried more than once. What should you investigate first?$qb$, $qb$What is different about Monday inputs, workload, or dependencies$qb$, $qb$Whether the Monday delay follows the same pattern each week$qb$, $qb$Whether the task deadline can move to Tuesday$qb$, $qb$Which person works slowest on Monday$qb$, $qb$How to add more people every Monday$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose a low-cost supplier for a trial, but one reference check is still pending. Waiting for complete information would also have a cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use a small reversible trial if current evidence is acceptable and review when the reference arrives$qb$, $qb$Assess how much the missing reference could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay the trial until every check is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier immediately$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier a colleague prefers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new shortcut saves five minutes in your task but creates ten minutes of extra checking for the next person. Improving one part should not simply move effort or risk to another part. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Consider the total process time and change the shortcut if it makes the overall flow worse$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra checking with the next person$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut because your own task is faster$qb$, $qb$Ask the next person to improve their checking method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it happens later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly meeting exists mainly because it has always been on the calendar. The current step consumes recurring team time, not just your own time. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What useful outcome is this meeting supposed to produce?$qb$, $qb$Who originally created the meeting?$qb$, $qb$How can we make the meeting shorter?$qb$, $qb$Can we move it to another day?$qb$, $qb$Who attends most often?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Five people send updates on the same issue. Two contain new facts; three repeat old information. The summary will be used to make a decision, not merely to record information. What is the best summary?$qb$, $qb$Highlight the new facts, current decision need, and any unresolved point$qb$, $qb$Summarize the two messages with new information$qb$, $qb$List all five updates in a shorter form$qb$, $qb$Give equal space to each person's update$qb$, $qb$Use only the latest message$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit reports that a new process improved productivity, but overtime increased during the same period. What should you examine?$qb$, $qb$Whether the productivity measure still shows improvement after accounting for overtime and other resource changes$qb$, $qb$Compare output per paid hour before and after the process change$qb$, $qb$Accept the productivity gain because output increased$qb$, $qb$Reject the claim because overtime increased$qb$, $qb$Use only the reported productivity percentage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several teams report different symptoms after a common system change. What is the best first approach?$qb$, $qb$Map the symptoms against the shared change and look for a common mechanism or dependency before fixing each symptom separately$qb$, $qb$Compare the incidents and identify when and where each began$qb$, $qb$Fix the most disruptive symptom first$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to solve its own issue$qb$, $qb$Roll back the system change immediately$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must commit some resources now, but an important assumption may change next month. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Stage the commitment, limit irreversible exposure, define the assumption to monitor, and set a clear trigger for changing direction$qb$, $qb$Commit only what is needed now and review when the assumption becomes clearer$qb$, $qb$Wait until next month before committing anything$qb$, $qb$Make the full commitment to avoid losing momentum$qb$, $qb$Choose the option with the highest upside$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change reduces processing cost in one team but increases error handling, customer calls, and waiting elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate total cost, customer impact, and flow performance, then redesign the change around the whole outcome$qb$, $qb$Map the downstream effects and compare total effort before and after$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because the original team's cost fell$qb$, $qb$Ask downstream teams to absorb the extra work$qb$, $qb$Reverse the change immediately without preserving any benefit$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A long-standing control is still performed manually, but the risk it was created for is now partly handled automatically. What is the best starting point?$qb$, $qb$Define the underlying risk and required assurance, then determine what control is still necessary in the current process$qb$, $qb$Review how often the manual control finds issues that automation misses$qb$, $qb$Remove the manual control because automation exists$qb$, $qb$Automate the manual control as well$qb$, $qb$Keep both controls because more checking is safer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must brief a decision-maker using operational data, customer feedback, and expert opinions that conflict on one important point. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Distinguish evidence quality, identify agreement and conflict, explain the uncertainty, and connect the strongest signals to the decision required$qb$, $qb$Summarize each source and explain where they disagree$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to all sources$qb$, $qb$Follow the expert with the most experience$qb$, $qb$Present the information without recommending a view$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows a large percentage increase, but the starting number was very small. The conclusion may influence a resource or process decision. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$The actual numbers as well as the percentage before judging the size of the change$qb$, $qb$Compare the starting and ending values$qb$, $qb$Focus on the percentage because it shows growth$qb$, $qb$Assume a large percentage means a large business impact$qb$, $qb$Ignore the starting value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team receives many questions about the same instruction. Previous quick fixes have not prevented the issue from returning. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Identify exactly which part people misunderstand and why before rewriting it$qb$, $qb$Review the repeated questions and find the common point$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the whole instruction immediately$qb$, $qb$Tell people to read more carefully$qb$, $qb$Add more text to every section$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must select a meeting tool today, and two options meet the basic need but one feature is still being tested. Waiting for certainty would also delay useful work. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose based on essential needs and keep the choice easy to revise if the untested feature matters$qb$, $qb$Compare the importance of the untested feature before deciding$qb$, $qb$Wait until the feature test is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the tool with more features$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever tool your colleague likes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new quality check reduces customer errors but creates a long queue before delivery. A local improvement may be moving effort to another part of the process. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure both error reduction and queue impact, then improve the flow without losing essential control$qb$, $qb$Find which part of the check causes the waiting and redesign it$qb$, $qb$Remove the check because the queue is visible$qb$, $qb$Keep the check unchanged because quality improved$qb$, $qb$Add more waiting time to the delivery promise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team creates a weekly summary from a daily report, but nobody can name a different decision the weekly summary supports. The activity consumes recurring capacity across the team. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What unique need does the weekly summary serve that the daily report does not?$qb$, $qb$Who reads the weekly summary?$qb$, $qb$How long does the summary take to prepare?$qb$, $qb$Can the summary be automated?$qb$, $qb$Can the weekly summary be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several facts about a delay, but only two explain what decision is needed now. The summary will be used to decide what happens next. What should your update emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The two decision-relevant facts, current impact, and the choice required$qb$, $qb$The key facts and the immediate impact$qb$, $qb$Every fact so nobody can say information was omitted$qb$, $qb$The most recent fact only$qb$, $qb$The fact provided by the most senior person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a change is successful because one important customer liked it. The conclusion may influence a process or investment choice. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Look for broader evidence before concluding the change works for customers generally$qb$, $qb$Ask how other customers responded$qb$, $qb$Accept the conclusion because the customer is important$qb$, $qb$Reject the conclusion because one customer is never useful evidence$qb$, $qb$Use the customer's opinion as the main measure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process has become slower, but no single step appears much slower than before. Previous quick fixes have not stopped the problem returning. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full flow and look for small delays, waiting, or repeated handoffs that add up$qb$, $qb$Compare current timing with earlier timing at each step$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the longest step$qb$, $qb$Ask people which step feels slowest$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose whether to start a small task now or tomorrow, but tomorrow's workload is uncertain. Waiting for certainty has a cost as well as acting with incomplete information. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use current priorities and the cost of delay to make a reasonable choice, then adjust if conditions change$qb$, $qb$Start the task now if doing so protects tomorrow's flexibility$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow's workload is known$qb$, $qb$Always start early regardless of priority$qb$, $qb$Ask a coworker to choose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A rule prevents one type of error but creates repeated manual exceptions elsewhere. A local gain may be creating cost elsewhere in the flow. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate both the prevented errors and exception cost, then improve the rule for the whole flow$qb$, $qb$Measure the exception pattern and discuss it with affected teams$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because it prevents errors$qb$, $qb$Add more people to handle exceptions$qb$, $qb$Remove the rule without checking the original risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A form requires three approvals for a low-risk request. The step consumes recurring capacity across several people. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What risk is each approval meant to control?$qb$, $qb$Which approval takes the longest?$qb$, $qb$Can all approvals happen at the same time?$qb$, $qb$Who added the third approval?$qb$, $qb$Can the request form be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to summarize a problem from twenty messages, many of which repeat the same facts. The summary will support an actual decision. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Extract the confirmed facts, key disagreement, current impact, and decision needed$qb$, $qb$Group repeated messages and summarize the newest information$qb$, $qb$Write a shorter version of all twenty messages$qb$, $qb$Quote only the most senior participants$qb$, $qb$Use the final message as the summary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate says a process change caused a recent drop in quality. The conclusion may influence a wider team decision. What should you check first?$qb$, $qb$Whether the evidence actually links the change to the quality drop$qb$, $qb$What data was used to reach the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Whether other teammates agree with the explanation$qb$, $qb$How confident the teammate sounds about the cause$qb$, $qb$Whether the explanation is easy to understand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer complaint keeps returning after quick fixes. Earlier quick fixes have not prevented recurrence. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Look for the root cause before choosing another fix$qb$, $qb$Compare the recent complaints for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Apply the quickest fix again while collecting more information$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague for their preferred solution$qb$, $qb$Treat each complaint separately because the details differ$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two low-risk options today, but one useful fact will arrive tomorrow. Waiting for certainty also carries a cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose a reversible step using current evidence and review it tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Identify how much the missing fact could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay all action until the missing fact arrives$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that worked last time$qb$, $qb$Ask someone senior to make the decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shortcut makes your team's work faster but creates extra work for another team. A local improvement may create cost elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the total impact before deciding whether to keep the shortcut$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra work with the affected team$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut if your own target improves$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to adjust their process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it belongs to another team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team follows a step only because 'we have always done it this way.' The step consumes recurring capacity across the team. What is the best question to ask?$qb$, $qb$What basic need is this step meant to satisfy?$qb$, $qb$When was this step first introduced?$qb$, $qb$Who originally approved this step?$qb$, $qb$Which teams still use this step?$qb$, $qb$Why does nobody want to change it?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have five updates about the same issue, but only two contain facts needed for a decision. The summary will be used to decide what happens next. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the decision-relevant facts and set aside the extra detail$qb$, $qb$Summarize all five updates before deciding$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to every update$qb$, $qb$Use the longest update because it has more detail$qb$, $qb$Choose the update from the most senior sender$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A chart shows sales rising after a new campaign started. The conclusion may influence a wider process decision. What should you check before saying the campaign caused the rise?$qb$, $qb$Whether other factors could also explain the increase$qb$, $qb$Whether the timing and data support a connection$qb$, $qb$Whether the campaign team agrees with the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Whether the chart looks convincing$qb$, $qb$Whether sales are higher than last month$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team keeps missing the same information during handovers. Previous quick fixes have not prevented recurrence. What is the best first improvement?$qb$, $qb$Find why the information is being missed and fix that point in the handoff$qb$, $qb$Review several missed handovers for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Add another reminder to the current process$qb$, $qb$Ask people to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Accept that handovers sometimes fail$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A low-cost decision must be made today, but your information is incomplete. Waiting for certainty also has a cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose the safest reversible option supported by current facts$qb$, $qb$Identify the biggest unknown and limit its possible impact$qb$, $qb$Wait until you have complete information$qb$, $qb$Choose what most coworkers would choose$qb$, $qb$Avoid deciding and let the situation resolve itself$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team reduces a checking step and finishes work faster, but customer corrections increase. A local gain may be creating cost elsewhere in the flow. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and balance speed with the cost of corrections$qb$, $qb$Restore the check while you examine the new error pattern$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster process and handle corrections separately$qb$, $qb$Ask customer support to manage the extra corrections$qb$, $qb$Judge success only by your team's faster completion time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly report takes two hours to prepare, but nobody can explain which decision it supports. The step consumes recurring team capacity. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What decision or need is this report meant to serve?$qb$, $qb$Which parts of the report take the most time?$qb$, $qb$Can the report be automated?$qb$, $qb$Who originally requested the report?$qb$, $qb$How can we prepare the same report faster?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a long email thread before a meeting. The summary will support an actual decision. What should you extract first?$qb$, $qb$The decision needed, key facts, open issues, and owners$qb$, $qb$The latest message and any attached files$qb$, $qb$A short summary of every message$qb$, $qb$Comments from the most senior people$qb$, $qb$Every detail in the order it was written$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer says a problem happens 'every time,' but you have only one recorded case. The conclusion may influence a wider decision. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check the records and gather enough evidence before accepting the claim as a pattern$qb$, $qb$Ask for examples of other times the problem occurred$qb$, $qb$Treat it as a repeated issue because the customer is confident$qb$, $qb$Assume the records are incomplete$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the claim because only one case is recorded$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report is often late because information arrives from several people at different times. Previous quick fixes have not stopped recurrence. What should you examine first?$qb$, $qb$Where the flow breaks and why the needed information arrives late$qb$, $qb$Which contributor is late most often$qb$, $qb$Whether a reminder email would solve the problem$qb$, $qb$Whether the report deadline can be moved$qb$, $qb$Who should be blamed for the delays$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose a meeting date before one person's availability is confirmed. Waiting for certainty also has a cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose the best provisional date, state the assumption, and adjust if needed$qb$, $qb$Hold the most likely date and confirm it when the person replies$qb$, $qb$Wait to schedule anything until everyone confirms$qb$, $qb$Pick a date without telling anyone it may change$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to choose so you are not responsible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change reduces your team's workload but causes more questions for customer support. A local improvement may be moving work elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure the total effect and adjust the change so the overall process improves$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra questions with customer support and identify the cause$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because your team is more efficient$qb$, $qb$Ask support to create a standard answer for the new questions$qb$, $qb$Ignore the effect because support owns customer questions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A form asks for information that nobody uses after submission. The activity consumes recurring capacity. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What purpose does this information serve?$qb$, $qb$Who added this field to the form?$qb$, $qb$How long does the field take to complete?$qb$, $qb$Can the field be made optional?$qb$, $qb$Can the form be automated?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to brief your manager after reading several customer comments. The summary will support a real decision. What is most useful to report?$qb$, $qb$The repeated themes, important exceptions, and evidence behind them$qb$, $qb$The three comments that best represent the overall feedback$qb$, $qb$A summary of every comment received$qb$, $qb$Only the strongest negative comment$qb$, $qb$Only comments from the largest customers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows a large percentage increase, but the starting number was very small. The conclusion may drive investment, so simple before-and-after comparison is not enough. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$The actual numbers as well as the percentage before judging the size of the change$qb$, $qb$Compare the starting and ending values$qb$, $qb$Focus on the percentage because it shows growth$qb$, $qb$Assume a large percentage means a large business impact$qb$, $qb$Ignore the starting value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team receives many questions about the same instruction. Different symptoms may come from one shared upstream condition. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Identify exactly which part people misunderstand and why before rewriting it$qb$, $qb$Review the repeated questions and find the common point$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the whole instruction immediately$qb$, $qb$Tell people to read more carefully$qb$, $qb$Add more text to every section$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must select a meeting tool today, and two options meet the basic need but one feature is still being tested. Both acting and waiting carry meaningful downside, and the decision cannot be delegated away. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose based on essential needs and keep the choice easy to revise if the untested feature matters$qb$, $qb$Compare the importance of the untested feature before deciding$qb$, $qb$Wait until the feature test is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the tool with more features$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever tool your colleague likes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new quality check reduces customer errors but creates a long queue before delivery. The local metric looks better, but customer and downstream effects suggest the total outcome may be worse. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure both error reduction and queue impact, then improve the flow without losing essential control$qb$, $qb$Find which part of the check causes the waiting and redesign it$qb$, $qb$Remove the check because the queue is visible$qb$, $qb$Keep the check unchanged because quality improved$qb$, $qb$Add more waiting time to the delivery promise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team creates a weekly summary from a daily report, but nobody can name a different decision the weekly summary supports. The process contains controls accumulated over years, while the original risks and technology have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What unique need does the weekly summary serve that the daily report does not?$qb$, $qb$Who reads the weekly summary?$qb$, $qb$How long does the summary take to prepare?$qb$, $qb$Can the summary be automated?$qb$, $qb$Can the weekly summary be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several facts about a delay, but only two explain what decision is needed now. Credible sources disagree because their definitions, assumptions, and time periods are different. What should your update emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The two decision-relevant facts, current impact, and the choice required$qb$, $qb$The key facts and the immediate impact$qb$, $qb$Every fact so nobody can say information was omitted$qb$, $qb$The most recent fact only$qb$, $qb$The fact provided by the most senior person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R3-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a change is successful because one important customer liked it. The conclusion could influence resource allocation, so alternative explanations matter. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Look for broader evidence before concluding the change works for customers generally$qb$, $qb$Ask how other customers responded$qb$, $qb$Accept the conclusion because the customer is important$qb$, $qb$Reject the conclusion because one customer is never useful evidence$qb$, $qb$Use the customer's opinion as the main measure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R3-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process has become slower, but no single step appears much slower than before. Several local fixes have treated symptoms without preventing recurrence. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full flow and look for small delays, waiting, or repeated handoffs that add up$qb$, $qb$Compare current timing with earlier timing at each step$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the longest step$qb$, $qb$Ask people which step feels slowest$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R3-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose whether to start a small task now or tomorrow, but tomorrow's workload is uncertain. Both acting and waiting have meaningful cost, and the decision remains yours. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use current priorities and the cost of delay to make a reasonable choice, then adjust if conditions change$qb$, $qb$Start the task now if doing so protects tomorrow's flexibility$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow's workload is known$qb$, $qb$Always start early regardless of priority$qb$, $qb$Ask a coworker to choose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R3-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A rule prevents one type of error but creates repeated manual exceptions elsewhere. A local efficiency gain may be shifting cost or risk downstream. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate both the prevented errors and exception cost, then improve the rule for the whole flow$qb$, $qb$Measure the exception pattern and discuss it with affected teams$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because it prevents errors$qb$, $qb$Add more people to handle exceptions$qb$, $qb$Remove the rule without checking the original risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R3-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A form requires three approvals for a low-risk request. The activity consumes recurring capacity and its original purpose may have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What risk is each approval meant to control?$qb$, $qb$Which approval takes the longest?$qb$, $qb$Can all approvals happen at the same time?$qb$, $qb$Who added the third approval?$qb$, $qb$Can the request form be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R3-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to summarize a problem from twenty messages, many of which repeat the same facts. The synthesis will be used to choose between competing actions. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Extract the confirmed facts, key disagreement, current impact, and decision needed$qb$, $qb$Group repeated messages and summarize the newest information$qb$, $qb$Write a shorter version of all twenty messages$qb$, $qb$Quote only the most senior participants$qb$, $qb$Use the final message as the summary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R4-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate says a process change caused a recent drop in quality. The conclusion may influence resource allocation, so alternative explanations matter. What should you check first?$qb$, $qb$Whether the evidence actually links the change to the quality drop$qb$, $qb$What data was used to reach the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Whether other teammates agree with the explanation$qb$, $qb$How confident the teammate sounds about the cause$qb$, $qb$Whether the explanation is easy to understand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R4-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer complaint keeps returning after quick fixes. Repeated local fixes have not prevented the pattern from returning. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Look for the root cause before choosing another fix$qb$, $qb$Compare the recent complaints for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Apply the quickest fix again while collecting more information$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague for their preferred solution$qb$, $qb$Treat each complaint separately because the details differ$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R4-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two low-risk options today, but one useful fact will arrive tomorrow. Both action and delay have meaningful downside, and the decision remains yours. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose a reversible step using current evidence and review it tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Identify how much the missing fact could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay all action until the missing fact arrives$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that worked last time$qb$, $qb$Ask someone senior to make the decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R4-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shortcut makes your team's work faster but creates extra work for another team. A local gain may be shifting cost, risk, or delay elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the total impact before deciding whether to keep the shortcut$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra work with the affected team$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut if your own target improves$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to adjust their process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it belongs to another team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
