  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team reduces a checking step and finishes work faster, but customer corrections increase. A local gain may be shifting cost, delay, or risk elsewhere. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and balance speed with the cost of corrections$qb$, $qb$Restore the check while you examine the new error pattern$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster process and handle corrections separately$qb$, $qb$Ask customer support to manage the extra corrections$qb$, $qb$Judge success only by your team's faster completion time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly report takes two hours to prepare, but nobody can explain which decision it supports. The activity consumes recurring capacity and may no longer support the original need. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What decision or need is this report meant to serve?$qb$, $qb$Which parts of the report take the most time?$qb$, $qb$Can the report be automated?$qb$, $qb$Who originally requested the report?$qb$, $qb$How can we prepare the same report faster?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a long email thread before a meeting. The synthesis will be used to choose between competing actions. What should you extract first?$qb$, $qb$The decision needed, key facts, open issues, and owners$qb$, $qb$The latest message and any attached files$qb$, $qb$A short summary of every message$qb$, $qb$Comments from the most senior people$qb$, $qb$Every detail in the order it was written$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A survey result is based on five responses from a team of one hundred. The conclusion may influence investment or resource allocation. What is the main concern?$qb$, $qb$The sample is too small to support a strong conclusion about the whole team$qb$, $qb$The five respondents may not represent the full team$qb$, $qb$The survey may contain poorly worded questions$qb$, $qb$The result may still be correct if all five agree$qb$, $qb$There is no concern if the percentage is high$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task is delayed every Monday but usually runs on time later in the week. Repeated local corrections have not removed the underlying pattern. What should you investigate first?$qb$, $qb$What is different about Monday inputs, workload, or dependencies$qb$, $qb$Whether the Monday delay follows the same pattern each week$qb$, $qb$Whether the task deadline can move to Tuesday$qb$, $qb$Which person works slowest on Monday$qb$, $qb$How to add more people every Monday$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose a low-cost supplier for a trial, but one reference check is still pending. Both action and delay carry material risk, and the decision cannot simply be passed upward. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use a small reversible trial if current evidence is acceptable and review when the reference arrives$qb$, $qb$Assess how much the missing reference could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay the trial until every check is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier immediately$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier a colleague prefers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new shortcut saves five minutes in your task but creates ten minutes of extra checking for the next person. A local improvement may be moving cost, risk, or delay elsewhere in the system. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Consider the total process time and change the shortcut if it makes the overall flow worse$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra checking with the next person$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut because your own task is faster$qb$, $qb$Ask the next person to improve their checking method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it happens later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly meeting exists mainly because it has always been on the calendar. The activity consumes recurring capacity and its original purpose may have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What useful outcome is this meeting supposed to produce?$qb$, $qb$Who originally created the meeting?$qb$, $qb$How can we make the meeting shorter?$qb$, $qb$Can we move it to another day?$qb$, $qb$Who attends most often?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Five people send updates on the same issue. Two contain new facts; three repeat old information. The synthesis will be used to choose between competing courses of action. What is the best summary?$qb$, $qb$Highlight the new facts, current decision need, and any unresolved point$qb$, $qb$Summarize the two messages with new information$qb$, $qb$List all five updates in a shorter form$qb$, $qb$Give equal space to each person's update$qb$, $qb$Use only the latest message$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report shows complaints fell after a change, but total customer volume also fell. The conclusion may influence resource allocation, so alternative explanations need to be tested. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$Whether the complaint rate changed after accounting for customer volume$qb$, $qb$Compare complaints as a percentage of customer activity$qb$, $qb$Conclude the change worked because complaints are lower$qb$, $qb$Ignore customer volume because complaint count is what matters$qb$, $qb$Use whichever measure gives the better result$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team fixes the same data error every week. Repeated local fixes have not prevented the pattern from returning. What is the best first step toward a lasting solution?$qb$, $qb$Trace where the error first enters the process and why$qb$, $qb$Review several cases to confirm the same source$qb$, $qb$Create a faster correction method$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to fix the error each week$qb$, $qb$Accept the error as part of the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to run a small test today, but one minor cost estimate is uncertain. Both acting and waiting carry meaningful downside, and the decision remains yours. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Set a safe cost limit, run a reversible test, and update the decision when the estimate is known$qb$, $qb$Assess the highest reasonable cost before starting$qb$, $qb$Wait until the exact cost is confirmed$qb$, $qb$Use the lowest possible cost estimate$qb$, $qb$Cancel the test because information is incomplete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A faster intake process increases the number of incomplete requests reaching the next team. A local improvement may be shifting cost, delay, or risk elsewhere in the system. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and improve intake speed without passing poor-quality work downstream$qb$, $qb$Measure which missing details create the most rework and adjust intake$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster intake and let the next team reject incomplete requests$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the next team$qb$, $qb$Ignore completeness because intake speed improved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team manually copies data into a second file only because the second file has always existed. The activity consumes recurring capacity and its original purpose may no longer apply. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What need does the second file actually serve?$qb$, $qb$Who created the second file?$qb$, $qb$How quickly can the copying be done?$qb$, $qb$Can a macro copy the data automatically?$qb$, $qb$Who is responsible for maintaining the file?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have ten comments about a product issue, but seven repeat the same point. The synthesis will be used to choose between competing actions. What is the best summary?$qb$, $qb$Report the repeated theme once, its frequency, and the important different points$qb$, $qb$Group similar comments and mention the main exception$qb$, $qb$List all ten comments in shorter form$qb$, $qb$Select the three strongest comments$qb$, $qb$Use only the newest comments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says a training session improved performance because scores rose afterward. The conclusion may influence resource allocation, so alternative explanations matter. What else should you consider?$qb$, $qb$Whether other changes could explain the higher scores and whether the comparison is fair$qb$, $qb$How scores were measured before and after the training$qb$, $qb$Whether participants liked the training$qb$, $qb$Whether the trainer is experienced$qb$, $qb$Whether the improvement sounds reasonable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer form is often returned because one field is left empty. Repeated local fixes have not prevented recurrence. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Find out why users miss the field before choosing a fix$qb$, $qb$Review examples and ask users what confused them$qb$, $qb$Make the field mandatory immediately$qb$, $qb$Add more instructions to the whole form$qb$, $qb$Blame users for not reading carefully$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small purchase must be made today, but two suppliers have similar offers and one detail is unknown. Both acting and waiting carry meaningful downside, and the decision remains yours. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose using the important known criteria and keep the decision easy to adjust if the missing detail matters$qb$, $qb$Identify whether the missing detail could change the preferred supplier$qb$, $qb$Delay the purchase until every detail is known$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier you used last time$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier without further review$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team automates one step and saves time, but the next team receives more exceptions to handle manually. A local improvement may be shifting cost, delay, or risk elsewhere in the system. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure the total effect and redesign the automation so it does not simply move work downstream$qb$, $qb$Review the exceptions with the next team and identify why they increased$qb$, $qb$Keep the automation because your team's time saving is clear$qb$, $qb$Ask the next team to automate their exception handling$qb$, $qb$Ignore the exceptions because they are outside your process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A daily tracker asks for six fields, but only two are used in any decision. The activity consumes recurring capacity and its original purpose may no longer apply. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what each field is meant to achieve before deciding what to remove$qb$, $qb$Identify which fields take longest to complete$qb$, $qb$Delete the four fields that appear unused$qb$, $qb$Ask who originally added the fields$qb$, $qb$Automate all six fields$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have data, customer comments, and a manager's opinion about the same problem. The synthesis will be used to choose between competing actions. What is the best way to brief a decision?$qb$, $qb$Separate evidence from opinion, highlight the strongest signals, and state what remains uncertain$qb$, $qb$Summarize the data and comments, then note the manager's view$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to all three sources$qb$, $qb$Lead with the manager's opinion$qb$, $qb$Present everything without prioritizing it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a change is successful because one important customer liked it. The conclusion may drive significant investment or policy, so causal claims and evidence quality must withstand challenge. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Look for broader evidence before concluding the change works for customers generally$qb$, $qb$Ask how other customers responded$qb$, $qb$Accept the conclusion because the customer is important$qb$, $qb$Reject the conclusion because one customer is never useful evidence$qb$, $qb$Use the customer's opinion as the main measure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process has become slower, but no single step appears much slower than before. Different teams are treating symptoms locally, but the failures may share a systemic cause. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full flow and look for small delays, waiting, or repeated handoffs that add up$qb$, $qb$Compare current timing with earlier timing at each step$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the longest step$qb$, $qb$Ask people which step feels slowest$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose whether to start a small task now or tomorrow, but tomorrow's workload is uncertain. The decision is material, uncertainty will persist, and both delay and irreversible commitment carry significant cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use current priorities and the cost of delay to make a reasonable choice, then adjust if conditions change$qb$, $qb$Start the task now if doing so protects tomorrow's flexibility$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow's workload is known$qb$, $qb$Always start early regardless of priority$qb$, $qb$Ask a coworker to choose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A rule prevents one type of error but creates repeated manual exceptions elsewhere. A local performance gain may be creating larger customer, risk, cost, or capacity consequences elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate both the prevented errors and exception cost, then improve the rule for the whole flow$qb$, $qb$Measure the exception pattern and discuss it with affected teams$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because it prevents errors$qb$, $qb$Add more people to handle exceptions$qb$, $qb$Remove the rule without checking the original risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A form requires three approvals for a low-risk request. The governance activity has accumulated over years while risks, technology, and decision needs have materially changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What risk is each approval meant to control?$qb$, $qb$Which approval takes the longest?$qb$, $qb$Can all approvals happen at the same time?$qb$, $qb$Who added the third approval?$qb$, $qb$Can the request form be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to summarize a problem from twenty messages, many of which repeat the same facts. The synthesis will inform a high-impact choice where credible evidence sources disagree. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Extract the confirmed facts, key disagreement, current impact, and decision needed$qb$, $qb$Group repeated messages and summarize the newest information$qb$, $qb$Write a shorter version of all twenty messages$qb$, $qb$Quote only the most senior participants$qb$, $qb$Use the final message as the summary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows a large percentage increase, but the starting number was very small. The conclusion may drive significant investment or policy, so causal claims and evidence quality must withstand challenge. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$The actual numbers as well as the percentage before judging the size of the change$qb$, $qb$Compare the starting and ending values$qb$, $qb$Focus on the percentage because it shows growth$qb$, $qb$Assume a large percentage means a large business impact$qb$, $qb$Ignore the starting value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team receives many questions about the same instruction. Different teams are treating symptoms locally, while the failures may share a systemic upstream cause. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Identify exactly which part people misunderstand and why before rewriting it$qb$, $qb$Review the repeated questions and find the common point$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the whole instruction immediately$qb$, $qb$Tell people to read more carefully$qb$, $qb$Add more text to every section$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must select a meeting tool today, and two options meet the basic need but one feature is still being tested. The decision is material, uncertainty will persist, and both delay and irreversible commitment carry significant cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose based on essential needs and keep the choice easy to revise if the untested feature matters$qb$, $qb$Compare the importance of the untested feature before deciding$qb$, $qb$Wait until the feature test is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the tool with more features$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever tool your colleague likes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new quality check reduces customer errors but creates a long queue before delivery. A local performance gain may be creating larger customer, risk, cost, or capacity consequences elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure both error reduction and queue impact, then improve the flow without losing essential control$qb$, $qb$Find which part of the check causes the waiting and redesign it$qb$, $qb$Remove the check because the queue is visible$qb$, $qb$Keep the check unchanged because quality improved$qb$, $qb$Add more waiting time to the delivery promise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team creates a weekly summary from a daily report, but nobody can name a different decision the weekly summary supports. The governance activity has accumulated over years while technology, risks, and decision needs have materially changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What unique need does the weekly summary serve that the daily report does not?$qb$, $qb$Who reads the weekly summary?$qb$, $qb$How long does the summary take to prepare?$qb$, $qb$Can the summary be automated?$qb$, $qb$Can the weekly summary be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several facts about a delay, but only two explain what decision is needed now. The synthesis will inform a high-impact choice where credible evidence sources disagree. What should your update emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The two decision-relevant facts, current impact, and the choice required$qb$, $qb$The key facts and the immediate impact$qb$, $qb$Every fact so nobody can say information was omitted$qb$, $qb$The most recent fact only$qb$, $qb$The fact provided by the most senior person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The analysis will influence major investment, while the apparent improvement could also be explained by changes in volume and resources. How does a critical thinker objectively evaluate vendor claims regarding software efficiency gains?$qb$, $qb$Scrutinizing raw performance data, assessing workflow compatibility, and requesting structured trials.$qb$, $qb$Presenting vendor claims to leadership, highlighting positive client testimonials and advertised benefits.$qb$, $qb$Requesting vendor pricing discounts before deciding whether to pilot the software tool.$qb$, $qb$Consulting other internal departments to inquire if they have utilized similar vendor tools in past projects.$qb$, $qb$Accepting vendor statistics as valid industry benchmarks and recommending software adoption.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Different functions see different symptoms after the same upstream change and are independently funding local fixes. What is the most effective approach to identify root causes when service satisfaction scores decline?$qb$, $qb$Deconstructing ticket lifecycle metrics, mapping processing stages, and testing targeted workflow fixes.$qb$, $qb$Holding brainstorming sessions with service agents to gather qualitative opinions on service delays.$qb$, $qb$Establishing daily ticket processing quotas for agents to raise overall operational speed.$qb$, $qb$Escalating satisfaction drops to leadership with recommendations for temporary staffing additions.$qb$, $qb$Sending follow-up survey forms to dissatisfied customers to collect additional individual complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Uncertainty will remain for months; delaying loses a strategic window, while a full commitment would be difficult to reverse. How should a project lead make operational decisions when choosing between delivery options with incomplete data?$qb$, $qb$Mitigating downside risks by evaluating potential client impact, selecting reliable options, and setting checks.$qb$, $qb$Selecting the fastest theoretical option to maximize speed potential, monitoring progress along the way.$qb$, $qb$Delaying operational choices for several hours to wait for updated background data reports.$qb$, $qb$Asking the client to evaluate risk trade-offs and choose their preferred delivery pathway.$qb$, $qb$Splitting resources equally across available delivery options to hedge operational risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process change makes one step faster but causes more errors later in the workflow. One function's target has improved sharply while customer effort, downstream cost, and operational risk have all risen. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluating systemic trade-offs, restoring essential quality checks, and redesigning workflows to balance speed.$qb$, $qb$Adding warning documentation to deliverables advising clients to verify contents upon receipt.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining high processing speeds while establishing dedicated fast-track teams to handle downstream errors.$qb$, $qb$Reverting strictly to original slower workflows to eliminate downstream operational errors completely.$qb$, $qb$Transferring downstream error tracking metrics to support teams to handle post-delivery complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team still performs a copy-paste reconciliation step because it has always been part of the process. The governance process contains years of accumulated controls, although technology, risk exposure, and decision needs have changed. What is the best way to decide whether the step is still needed?$qb$, $qb$Deconstructing underlying data inputs and reporting needs, eliminating redundant steps, and automating flow.$qb$, $qb$Searching for advanced spreadsheet macros to execute existing copy-paste steps more quickly.$qb$, $qb$Dividing manual copy-paste duties among multiple team members to reduce individual time burden.$qb$, $qb$Recommending that reconciliation task frequencies be reduced from weekly to monthly to save hours.$qb$, $qb$Executing the process exactly as documented to maintain historical tracking continuity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Leaders need one recommendation from credible sources that disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons. What is the primary goal when synthesizing a dense multi-page industry report into an executive briefing?$qb$, $qb$Distilling critical strategic signals, removing operational noise, and presenting actionable insights.$qb$, $qb$Copying key executive summary sections and visual data charts directly into a condensed document.$qb$, $qb$Creating a detailed section-by-section outline covering all topics presented in the original report.$qb$, $qb$Presenting raw numerical data tables exclusively, omitting narrative context to keep briefings short.$qb$, $qb$Compiling individual chapter summaries written by department specialists into a unified document.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate says a process change caused a recent drop in quality. The conclusion could drive major investment, while changes in volume, staffing, and timing offer plausible alternative explanations. What should you check first?$qb$, $qb$Whether the evidence actually links the change to the quality drop$qb$, $qb$What data was used to reach the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Whether other teammates agree with the explanation$qb$, $qb$How confident the teammate sounds about the cause$qb$, $qb$Whether the explanation is easy to understand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer complaint keeps returning after quick fixes. Several functions see different symptoms after one upstream change and are funding separate fixes. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Look for the root cause before choosing another fix$qb$, $qb$Compare the recent complaints for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Apply the quickest fix again while collecting more information$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague for their preferred solution$qb$, $qb$Treat each complaint separately because the details differ$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two low-risk options today, but one useful fact will arrive tomorrow. Uncertainty will remain for months; waiting loses a strategic opportunity while full commitment would be hard to reverse. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose a reversible step using current evidence and review it tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Identify how much the missing fact could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay all action until the missing fact arrives$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that worked last time$qb$, $qb$Ask someone senior to make the decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shortcut makes your team's work faster but creates extra work for another team. One function's metric is improving while total cost, customer effort, and operational risk are worsening. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the total impact before deciding whether to keep the shortcut$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra work with the affected team$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut if your own target improves$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to adjust their process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it belongs to another team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team follows a step only because 'we have always done it this way.' The governance process has accumulated controls for historical incidents even though technology and risk exposure have changed. What is the best question to ask?$qb$, $qb$What basic need is this step meant to satisfy?$qb$, $qb$When was this step first introduced?$qb$, $qb$Who originally approved this step?$qb$, $qb$Which teams still use this step?$qb$, $qb$Why does nobody want to change it?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have five updates about the same issue, but only two contain facts needed for a decision. Credible sources disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons, yet leaders need one recommendation. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the decision-relevant facts and set aside the extra detail$qb$, $qb$Summarize all five updates before deciding$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to every update$qb$, $qb$Use the longest update because it has more detail$qb$, $qb$Choose the update from the most senior sender$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A chart shows sales rising after a new campaign started. The conclusion could drive major investment while changes in volume, staffing, and timing offer plausible alternative explanations. What should you check before saying the campaign caused the rise?$qb$, $qb$Whether other factors could also explain the increase$qb$, $qb$Whether the timing and data support a connection$qb$, $qb$Whether the campaign team agrees with the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Whether the chart looks convincing$qb$, $qb$Whether sales are higher than last month$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team keeps missing the same information during handovers. Several functions see different symptoms after one upstream change and are independently funding local fixes. What is the best first improvement?$qb$, $qb$Find why the information is being missed and fix that point in the handoff$qb$, $qb$Review several missed handovers for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Add another reminder to the current process$qb$, $qb$Ask people to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Accept that handovers sometimes fail$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A low-cost decision must be made today, but your information is incomplete. Uncertainty will persist for months; waiting loses a strategic window while full commitment would be difficult to reverse. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose the safest reversible option supported by current facts$qb$, $qb$Identify the biggest unknown and limit its possible impact$qb$, $qb$Wait until you have complete information$qb$, $qb$Choose what most coworkers would choose$qb$, $qb$Avoid deciding and let the situation resolve itself$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team reduces a checking step and finishes work faster, but customer corrections increase. One function's metric is improving while total cost, customer effort, and operational risk are worsening. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and balance speed with the cost of corrections$qb$, $qb$Restore the check while you examine the new error pattern$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster process and handle corrections separately$qb$, $qb$Ask customer support to manage the extra corrections$qb$, $qb$Judge success only by your team's faster completion time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly report takes two hours to prepare, but nobody can explain which decision it supports. The governance process has accumulated controls for historical incidents although technology and risk exposure have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What decision or need is this report meant to serve?$qb$, $qb$Which parts of the report take the most time?$qb$, $qb$Can the report be automated?$qb$, $qb$Who originally requested the report?$qb$, $qb$How can we prepare the same report faster?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a long email thread before a meeting. Credible sources disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons, yet leaders need one recommendation. What should you extract first?$qb$, $qb$The decision needed, key facts, open issues, and owners$qb$, $qb$The latest message and any attached files$qb$, $qb$A short summary of every message$qb$, $qb$Comments from the most senior people$qb$, $qb$Every detail in the order it was written$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A survey result is based on five responses from a team of one hundred. The conclusion could drive major investment while changes in volume, staffing, and timing provide plausible alternative explanations. What is the main concern?$qb$, $qb$The sample is too small to support a strong conclusion about the whole team$qb$, $qb$The five respondents may not represent the full team$qb$, $qb$The survey may contain poorly worded questions$qb$, $qb$The result may still be correct if all five agree$qb$, $qb$There is no concern if the percentage is high$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task is delayed every Monday but usually runs on time later in the week. Several functions see different symptoms after one upstream change and are independently funding local fixes. What should you investigate first?$qb$, $qb$What is different about Monday inputs, workload, or dependencies$qb$, $qb$Whether the Monday delay follows the same pattern each week$qb$, $qb$Whether the task deadline can move to Tuesday$qb$, $qb$Which person works slowest on Monday$qb$, $qb$How to add more people every Monday$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose a low-cost supplier for a trial, but one reference check is still pending. Uncertainty will persist for months; waiting loses a strategic window while full commitment would be difficult to reverse. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use a small reversible trial if current evidence is acceptable and review when the reference arrives$qb$, $qb$Assess how much the missing reference could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay the trial until every check is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier immediately$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier a colleague prefers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new shortcut saves five minutes in your task but creates ten minutes of extra checking for the next person. One function's metric is improving while total cost, customer effort, and operational risk are worsening. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Consider the total process time and change the shortcut if it makes the overall flow worse$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra checking with the next person$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut because your own task is faster$qb$, $qb$Ask the next person to improve their checking method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it happens later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly meeting exists mainly because it has always been on the calendar. The governance process has accumulated controls for historical incidents although technology and risk exposure have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What useful outcome is this meeting supposed to produce?$qb$, $qb$Who originally created the meeting?$qb$, $qb$How can we make the meeting shorter?$qb$, $qb$Can we move it to another day?$qb$, $qb$Who attends most often?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Five people send updates on the same issue. Two contain new facts; three repeat old information. Credible sources disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons, yet leaders require one recommendation. What is the best summary?$qb$, $qb$Highlight the new facts, current decision need, and any unresolved point$qb$, $qb$Summarize the two messages with new information$qb$, $qb$List all five updates in a shorter form$qb$, $qb$Give equal space to each person's update$qb$, $qb$Use only the latest message$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report shows complaints fell after a change, but total customer volume also fell. The conclusion could drive major investment while changes in volume, staffing, and timing provide plausible alternative explanations. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$Whether the complaint rate changed after accounting for customer volume$qb$, $qb$Compare complaints as a percentage of customer activity$qb$, $qb$Conclude the change worked because complaints are lower$qb$, $qb$Ignore customer volume because complaint count is what matters$qb$, $qb$Use whichever measure gives the better result$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team fixes the same data error every week. Several functions see different symptoms after one upstream change and are independently funding local fixes. What is the best first step toward a lasting solution?$qb$, $qb$Trace where the error first enters the process and why$qb$, $qb$Review several cases to confirm the same source$qb$, $qb$Create a faster correction method$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to fix the error each week$qb$, $qb$Accept the error as part of the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to run a small test today, but one minor cost estimate is uncertain. Uncertainty will persist for months; waiting loses a strategic window while full commitment would be difficult to reverse. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Set a safe cost limit, run a reversible test, and update the decision when the estimate is known$qb$, $qb$Assess the highest reasonable cost before starting$qb$, $qb$Wait until the exact cost is confirmed$qb$, $qb$Use the lowest possible cost estimate$qb$, $qb$Cancel the test because information is incomplete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A faster intake process increases the number of incomplete requests reaching the next team. One function's metric is improving while total cost, customer effort, and operational risk are worsening. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and improve intake speed without passing poor-quality work downstream$qb$, $qb$Measure which missing details create the most rework and adjust intake$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster intake and let the next team reject incomplete requests$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the next team$qb$, $qb$Ignore completeness because intake speed improved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team manually copies data into a second file only because the second file has always existed. The governance process has accumulated controls for historical incidents although technology and risk exposure have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What need does the second file actually serve?$qb$, $qb$Who created the second file?$qb$, $qb$How quickly can the copying be done?$qb$, $qb$Can a macro copy the data automatically?$qb$, $qb$Who is responsible for maintaining the file?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have ten comments about a product issue, but seven repeat the same point. Credible sources disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons, yet leaders require one recommendation. What is the best summary?$qb$, $qb$Report the repeated theme once, its frequency, and the important different points$qb$, $qb$Group similar comments and mention the main exception$qb$, $qb$List all ten comments in shorter form$qb$, $qb$Select the three strongest comments$qb$, $qb$Use only the newest comments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says a training session improved performance because scores rose afterward. The conclusion could drive major investment while changes in volume, staffing, and timing provide plausible alternative explanations. What else should you consider?$qb$, $qb$Whether other changes could explain the higher scores and whether the comparison is fair$qb$, $qb$How scores were measured before and after the training$qb$, $qb$Whether participants liked the training$qb$, $qb$Whether the trainer is experienced$qb$, $qb$Whether the improvement sounds reasonable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer form is often returned because one field is left empty. Several functions see different symptoms after one upstream change and are independently funding local fixes. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Find out why users miss the field before choosing a fix$qb$, $qb$Review examples and ask users what confused them$qb$, $qb$Make the field mandatory immediately$qb$, $qb$Add more instructions to the whole form$qb$, $qb$Blame users for not reading carefully$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small purchase must be made today, but two suppliers have similar offers and one detail is unknown. Uncertainty will persist for months; waiting loses a strategic window while full commitment would be difficult to reverse. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose using the important known criteria and keep the decision easy to adjust if the missing detail matters$qb$, $qb$Identify whether the missing detail could change the preferred supplier$qb$, $qb$Delay the purchase until every detail is known$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier you used last time$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier without further review$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team automates one step and saves time, but the next team receives more exceptions to handle manually. One function's metric is improving while total cost, customer effort, and operational risk are worsening. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure the total effect and redesign the automation so it does not simply move work downstream$qb$, $qb$Review the exceptions with the next team and identify why they increased$qb$, $qb$Keep the automation because your team's time saving is clear$qb$, $qb$Ask the next team to automate their exception handling$qb$, $qb$Ignore the exceptions because they are outside your process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A daily tracker asks for six fields, but only two are used in any decision. The governance process has accumulated controls for historical incidents although technology and risk exposure have changed. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what each field is meant to achieve before deciding what to remove$qb$, $qb$Identify which fields take longest to complete$qb$, $qb$Delete the four fields that appear unused$qb$, $qb$Ask who originally added the fields$qb$, $qb$Automate all six fields$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have data, customer comments, and a manager's opinion about the same problem. Credible sources disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons, yet leaders require one recommendation. What is the best way to brief a decision?$qb$, $qb$Separate evidence from opinion, highlight the strongest signals, and state what remains uncertain$qb$, $qb$Summarize the data and comments, then note the manager's view$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to all three sources$qb$, $qb$Lead with the manager's opinion$qb$, $qb$Present everything without prioritizing it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows a large percentage increase, but the starting number was very small. The conclusion could drive major investment while changes in volume, staffing, and timing provide plausible alternative explanations. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$The actual numbers as well as the percentage before judging the size of the change$qb$, $qb$Compare the starting and ending values$qb$, $qb$Focus on the percentage because it shows growth$qb$, $qb$Assume a large percentage means a large business impact$qb$, $qb$Ignore the starting value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team receives many questions about the same instruction. Several functions see different symptoms after one upstream change and are independently funding local fixes. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Identify exactly which part people misunderstand and why before rewriting it$qb$, $qb$Review the repeated questions and find the common point$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the whole instruction immediately$qb$, $qb$Tell people to read more carefully$qb$, $qb$Add more text to every section$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must select a meeting tool today, and two options meet the basic need but one feature is still being tested. Uncertainty will persist; waiting loses a strategic window while full commitment would be difficult to reverse. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose based on essential needs and keep the choice easy to revise if the untested feature matters$qb$, $qb$Compare the importance of the untested feature before deciding$qb$, $qb$Wait until the feature test is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the tool with more features$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever tool your colleague likes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new quality check reduces customer errors but creates a long queue before delivery. One function's metric is improving while total cost, customer effort, and operational risk are worsening. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure both error reduction and queue impact, then improve the flow without losing essential control$qb$, $qb$Find which part of the check causes the waiting and redesign it$qb$, $qb$Remove the check because the queue is visible$qb$, $qb$Keep the check unchanged because quality improved$qb$, $qb$Add more waiting time to the delivery promise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team creates a weekly summary from a daily report, but nobody can name a different decision the weekly summary supports. The governance process has accumulated controls for historical incidents although technology and risk exposure have changed. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What unique need does the weekly summary serve that the daily report does not?$qb$, $qb$Who reads the weekly summary?$qb$, $qb$How long does the summary take to prepare?$qb$, $qb$Can the summary be automated?$qb$, $qb$Can the weekly summary be shorter?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several facts about a delay, but only two explain what decision is needed now. Credible sources disagree on definitions, assumptions, and time horizons, yet leaders require one recommendation. What should your update emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The two decision-relevant facts, current impact, and the choice required$qb$, $qb$The key facts and the immediate impact$qb$, $qb$Every fact so nobody can say information was omitted$qb$, $qb$The most recent fact only$qb$, $qb$The fact provided by the most senior person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business case estimates major savings from consolidating vendors. The model assumes every business unit will adopt the new contract immediately. What is the strongest review?$qb$, $qb$Test adoption realism, transition costs, exceptions, switching risks and sensitivity before accepting the headline savings.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to reduce the savings estimate by a standard contingency percentage.$qb$, $qb$Compare the estimate with savings achieved in other consolidation programs.$qb$, $qb$Approve the case if the arithmetic in the model is correct.$qb$, $qb$Reject the case because organization-wide adoption is never realistic.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process has a high rework rate, but teams disagree whether the cause is training, system design, or unclear policy. What should happen before choosing a solution?$qb$, $qb$Trace where rework originates, segment the failure patterns, test competing hypotheses, and target the dominant causes.$qb$, $qb$Run refresher training because it is the quickest intervention to test.$qb$, $qb$Upgrade the system because repeated rework usually indicates poor tooling.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to vote on which cause appears most likely.$qb$, $qb$Introduce stricter quality checks at the end of the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to enter a market before a competitor. Demand evidence is promising, regulation is still evolving, and delay may forfeit first-mover advantage. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Define the uncertainties that matter most, cap irreversible exposure, stage the commitment, and predefine conditions for scaling, pausing, or exiting.$qb$, $qb$Enter at full scale because early advantage is difficult to recover later.$qb$, $qb$Wait until regulation and demand are both sufficiently clear.$qb$, $qb$Run a small pilot but avoid setting exit criteria until more is learned.$qb$, $qb$Follow the competitor's decision once their market response becomes visible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A company improves call-handling time by simplifying agent scripts, but complaint recurrence rises and back-office workload increases. What is the best interpretation?$qb$, $qb$The local metric improved while the wider system absorbed cost; evaluate total customer outcome, rework and cross-process effects before judging success.$qb$, $qb$The script change should be retained because the primary service metric improved.$qb$, $qb$The back office needs efficiency improvements to absorb the higher workload.$qb$, $qb$Agents should receive more coaching so complaints do not recur.$qb$, $qb$The two changes are unrelated unless direct causation is proven.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team argues that a service must keep a seven-step approval because 'risk requires it.' No one can explain which risk each step controls. What is the best way to challenge the design?$qb$, $qb$Define the unacceptable outcomes first, identify controls actually needed for those risks, and rebuild the approval path from that requirement.$qb$, $qb$Remove the two slowest approvals and monitor whether incidents increase.$qb$, $qb$Benchmark how many approvals comparable organizations use.$qb$, $qb$Keep all seven steps until Risk formally approves a reduction.$qb$, $qb$Automate the existing approvals so the process becomes faster without redesign.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A 60-page report contains survey results, financial trends, operational incidents and conflicting stakeholder views. Executives need a decision in 20 minutes. What should you prioritize?$qb$, $qb$The few facts that change the decision, material uncertainties, credible counterevidence, and the implications of each viable option.$qb$, $qb$A balanced summary of every major section so no source is underrepresented.$qb$, $qb$The most recent data because older information is less relevant.$qb$, $qb$The financial analysis because executives generally prioritize economics.$qb$, $qb$The strongest evidence supporting your preferred recommendation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An investment paper cites strong growth, favorable analyst commentary and successful pilots, but its downside case assumes no competitor response. What is the most important challenge?$qb$, $qb$Test causal assumptions and missing adversarial scenarios, especially competitor behavior, adoption limits and conditions under which the thesis fails.$qb$, $qb$Ask for more analyst reports to confirm external sentiment.$qb$, $qb$Focus on whether the pilots achieved their objectives.$qb$, $qb$Apply a larger financial contingency and proceed.$qb$, $qb$Accept the case because several evidence sources are positive.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring customer failure survived several improvement projects; each fixed a symptom, yet the failure returns. What is the best next move?$qb$, $qb$Reframe the problem across the full system, identify recurring conditions and incentives, then test interventions aimed at the mechanism rather than the latest symptom.$qb$, $qb$Launch another project led by a more senior manager.$qb$, $qb$Increase compliance audits around the latest failure point.$qb$, $qb$Benchmark a competitor's process and copy its controls.$qb$, $qb$Replace the team responsible for the most recent incident.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between a partnership with proven economics but low future flexibility and a less-proven option preserving access to a potentially dominant ecosystem. What method is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Compare expected value, option value, reversibility and failure exposure; identify evidence that could change the choice and who owns residual risk.$qb$, $qb$Choose proven economics because demonstrated returns should dominate.$qb$, $qb$Choose flexibility because future adaptability matters most.$qb$, $qb$Split investment equally to benefit from both possibilities.$qb$, $qb$Delay until one option is clearly superior on every major dimension.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cost-cutting initiative reduces local staffing, increasing central support demand, slowing product changes and raising churn while each function still reports savings. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Shift evaluation to end-to-end economics and feedback effects, then redesign targets and decision rights so local savings cannot destroy enterprise value.$qb$, $qb$Increase central support budget to absorb extra demand.$qb$, $qb$Keep savings targets but add churn as a separate KPI.$qb$, $qb$Ask local leaders to coordinate more closely with central support.$qb$, $qb$Continue because each function is meeting its approved target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A seven-stage approval process exists because controls accumulated after unrelated incidents; nobody can explain why every stage is still needed. How should you redesign it?$qb$, $qb$Start from outcomes and risks that must be controlled, derive the minimum necessary checks, then validate the design against real failure modes.$qb$, $qb$Remove stages with the lowest rejection rates and monitor incidents.$qb$, $qb$Benchmark approval counts at comparable organizations.$qb$, $qb$Keep every stage unless the original control owner approves removal.$qb$, $qb$Automate all seven stages so the process becomes faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Finance, Sales, Operations and an external adviser provide conflicting forecasts before a capital decision. What should your executive synthesis emphasize?$qb$, $qb$Decision-relevant facts, why credible sources disagree, key assumptions and sensitivities, and uncertainty that remains material to the choice.$qb$, $qb$Average the four forecasts to create one neutral estimate.$qb$, $qb$Use the external adviser's forecast because it is independent.$qb$, $qb$Use the most conservative forecast to protect the organization.$qb$, $qb$Present all forecast details equally and let executives interpret them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic review cites ten favorable market indicators but omits two credible signals that could invalidate the growth thesis. What should concern you most?$qb$, $qb$Test the thesis against the omitted signals and specify what evidence would cause the organization to change course.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A company repeatedly fixes late deliveries by adding expediting staff, yet delays return whenever volume rises. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine constraints, variability, incentives and flow relationships that create recurring delay across the end-to-end system.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to exit a declining market. Current economics are weak, but a pending regulation could make your capabilities unusually valuable. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Compare exit, stay and option-preserving choices using probability, timing, reversibility and downside exposure, with signals for further commitment.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cost target causes teams to defer preventive maintenance, improving quarterly results but increasing failures elsewhere. What should executives change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign measures and accountability around lifecycle economics and cross-unit consequences so local savings cannot transfer risk.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A long-standing approval exists because 'the board once asked for it,' but nobody can identify the risk it controls. What is the strongest redesign method?$qb$, $qb$Define unacceptable outcomes and obligations first, determine what control is necessary, and retain approval only if it materially addresses that need.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Before an acquisition vote, market research, employee interviews, customer data and a consultant report point in different directions. What should your synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Organize evidence around critical assumptions, weight sources by relevance and reliability, expose contradictions, and show which uncertainties could change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An investment proposal relies on three assumptions supplied by a vendor that benefits from approval. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Independently test the assumptions capable of changing the decision, examine incentives and downside cases, and separate verified evidence from interested claims.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Complaints rise after a policy change, but service teams use manual exceptions to keep service metrics stable. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Trace the causal chain, hidden work and incentives to determine whether the policy creates systemic demand that headline metrics conceal.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must commit capital before a volatile market window closes, and several critical facts will remain unknowable. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Separate knowable from irreducible uncertainty, test what can be tested, price downside and reversibility, and make residual-risk ownership explicit.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A central efficiency program lowers unit cost but causes longer lead times, rework and lost sales downstream. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Govern end-to-end economics including feedback effects and transferred costs, then redesign incentives and operating choices around enterprise value.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An inherited process has nine mandatory reviews, each justified by a different historical incident. What is the strongest redesign method?$qb$, $qb$Start with current unacceptable outcomes and obligations, derive the controls actually required, and test whether each review materially reduces relevant risk.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Hundreds of pages of analysis contain contradictory forecasts and stakeholder claims before a board decision. What should your synthesis prioritize?$qb$, $qb$The facts and assumptions driving the decision, source reliability, meaningful contradictions, and what evidence could change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A market-entry case uses impressive averages, but outcomes vary dramatically by customer segment. What is the strongest analytical response?$qb$, $qb$Disaggregate the economics and drivers by segment, test whether the average masks loss-making or strategically different populations, and reassess the thesis.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Repeated quality failures occur only when demand spikes, although normal-period processes pass every audit. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine how capacity, queues, incentives, handoffs and controls behave under peak conditions to identify the mechanism that audits of normal operation miss.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose whether to fund a breakthrough technology with a small chance of very large value and a meaningful chance of failure. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate downside affordability, option value, learning milestones and staged commitments rather than forcing the opportunity into a single-point forecast.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A procurement saving increases supplier fragility, which then causes costly production interruptions. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Manage total system economics and resilience, including supplier health and interruption costs, and align procurement incentives with end-to-end value rather than purchase price.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A control requires three senior approvals because each approver historically checks the same evidence. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Define the risk and necessary independent judgments, eliminate redundant checks, and assign the minimum clear authorities needed to control the outcome.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An executive briefing contains strong evidence both for and against a proposed acquisition. What should the synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Organize evidence around the assumptions that determine value, weigh source quality, expose contradictions, and show which unresolved points could reverse the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposal shows a strong average return but combines one highly profitable region with three marginal ones. What should the decision-maker do?$qb$, $qb$Disaggregate value drivers and strategic rationale by region, test dependencies and alternatives, and decide whether bundling hides materially different investments.$qb$, $qb$At first, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$In this case, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring operational failure appears in different departments each quarter, so each incident review names a different local cause. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Look across incidents for common upstream conditions, incentives, interfaces or constraints that migrate the failure rather than treating each location as independent.$qb$, $qb$In this case, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You can acquire a scarce capability now at a high price or wait for organic development that may take three years. What is the strongest decision approach?$qb$, $qb$Compare strategic timing, capability quality, integration risk, opportunity cost and reversible alternatives, including staged partnerships, rather than comparing purchase price with build cost alone.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$In this case, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared technology platform reduces IT cost but forces business units into manual workarounds that are not counted centrally. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Measure total enterprise cost and service impact, surface transferred work, and adjust platform priorities and incentives based on end-to-end value.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$At first, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process requires a committee vote because a serious failure occurred ten years ago, but today's controls and technology are different. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconstruct the risk the vote was meant to control, assess current failure modes and safeguards, and retain only governance that adds material protection now.$qb$, $qb$At first, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$In this case, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy team gives you thirty indicators about an emerging market, many correlated and several contradictory. What should your synthesis focus on?$qb$, $qb$Identify the independent signals tied to critical assumptions, remove redundant noise, explain contradictions and define which movements would change the strategic choice.$qb$, $qb$In this case, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$At first, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposal claims a new channel caused revenue growth because both began in the same quarter. What is the strongest challenge?$qb$, $qb$Test alternative explanations, timing, customer mix and counterfactual evidence before attributing the growth to the channel.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Three functions experience the same type of error, but each local review identifies a different employee mistake. What should leadership examine?$qb$, $qb$Common process conditions, information flows, incentives or control design that make similar human errors likely across different functions.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You can enter a new market now with incomplete information or wait a year and risk losing scarce distribution partners. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Value the option created by early access, bound the downside, learn cheaply now, and stage commitment around evidence rather than choosing all-in versus wait.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and schedule the broader structural question for the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the resulting consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current course until the risk becomes certain enough to force action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A procurement policy minimizes unit price but causes larger inventory buffers, expedited freight and supplier failures elsewhere. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Manage total system economics and resilience, including downstream costs and feedback effects, and align procurement measures with enterprise value.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process includes four approvals inherited from earlier regulations that no longer apply. What is the strongest way to decide what remains?$qb$, $qb$Start from current obligations and unacceptable outcomes, derive the controls actually needed, and retain approvals only where they add distinct risk reduction.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board receives strong but conflicting evidence about a market entry from customers, regulators, internal teams and advisers. What should the executive synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Organize evidence around decision-critical assumptions, weight sources by relevance and reliability, expose contradictions, and show what could change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy paper highlights rapid market growth but uses revenue figures inflated by price increases during high inflation. What should you examine?$qb$, $qb$Separate nominal from real growth, volume, mix and price effects, then test whether the strategic opportunity remains attractive under the underlying demand economics.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek more stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several customer incidents occur at different stages of a process, but all happen near month-end. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Look for common month-end capacity, incentive, workload and control changes that could create failures across otherwise different process stages.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot but leave the success threshold and next decision undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new technology investment has uncertain commercial payoff but would teach capabilities needed for several future options. How should you assess it?$qb$, $qb$Value both direct economics and learning or option value, bound the downside, define learning milestones, and stage further investment on evidence.$qb$, $qb$Take a useful immediate step on the visible issue, but defer the wider structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared-service center hits its cost target by pushing complex work back to business units, where the effort is not measured. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Measure end-to-end cost and service outcomes, expose transferred work, and align ownership and incentives so reported efficiency reflects enterprise economics.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if risk or cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A control requires two committees to review identical evidence because of a historical merger compromise. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Identify the distinct risk judgments actually required, consolidate redundant review, and assign clear authority and escalation without preserving duplication for political history.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek more stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board pack includes hundreds of customer comments, market statistics and financial scenarios. What should the executive summary do?$qb$, $qb$Distill the evidence that bears on critical assumptions, explain source quality and contradictions, and show what could materially change the proposed decision.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot but leave the success threshold and next decision undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A growth proposal uses a large total addressable market but assumes your company can serve every segment equally well. What should you challenge?$qb$, $qb$Segment the market by customer need, competitive advantage, economics and realistic access, then estimate the portion the company can credibly win and serve profitably.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring service failure appears only after several upstream teams meet their individual month-end targets. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine how local target behavior changes queues, handoffs and workload downstream, and test whether the combined incentives create the recurring failure.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You can fund one of two uncertain innovations: one has higher expected return, the other creates capabilities useful across several future businesses. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Compare direct return, strategic option value, learning, downside affordability and portfolio fit, then stage investment where evidence can resolve uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A central purchasing team secures large discounts by consolidating suppliers, but business continuity risk rises sharply. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate total value including concentration and disruption exposure, set resilience requirements, and align procurement incentives with enterprise economics rather than discount alone.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A mandatory executive sign-off exists because of a failure under an old business model. What is the strongest way to reassess it?$qb$, $qb$Identify the current unacceptable outcome and failure modes, evaluate today's controls, and retain senior sign-off only if it adds distinct risk reduction beyond existing safeguards.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic review contains strong customer enthusiasm, weak unit economics and uncertain regulation. What should the executive synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Organize the evidence around the assumptions that determine viability, show where sources conflict, and identify which uncertainties can change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A market study reports 40% growth, but most growth comes from one temporary government subsidy. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate subsidized from underlying demand, test economics under plausible subsidy changes, and reassess whether the market thesis remains attractive without the temporary driver.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Repeated project delays occur in different functions, but all begin after shared specialists are allocated during quarterly planning. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine portfolio-level capacity assumptions, allocation rules and incentives around shared specialists rather than treating each project's delay as a local execution problem.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An uncertain acquisition could provide a capability valuable across several businesses even if the target's current product underperforms. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Separate standalone value from capability and option value, test integration and redeployment assumptions, and structure commitment around downside and strategic reuse.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A centralized planning process improves forecast consistency but slows local responses to sudden demand changes. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Separate decisions that need enterprise consistency from those needing local speed, design feedback and authority accordingly, and measure total service and economic impact.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An approval was introduced after a fraud incident, but modern automated controls now detect the same risk earlier. What is the strongest reassessment?$qb$, $qb$Compare the approval's incremental risk reduction with current controls and failure modes, and remove or redesign it if it no longer adds distinct protection.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board paper contains customer research, financial models and regulatory advice that support different conclusions. What should the synthesis emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The assumptions each evidence stream informs, source strength and contradictions, and which unresolved uncertainties could materially change the decision.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposal claims strong customer demand based on survey intent, but actual purchase behavior is limited. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Distinguish stated intent from demonstrated behavior, test willingness to pay and adoption barriers, and base the forecast on evidence closest to the actual decision.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Different business units suffer recurring shortages immediately after each annual budget reset, although their local causes differ. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine common budgeting assumptions, incentives, capacity decisions and timing effects that could systematically create shortages across units.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic project has modest direct returns but could prevent a competitor from controlling a critical industry standard. How should it be assessed?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate direct economics alongside strategic control, option value, downside and alternative ways to influence the standard, then stage commitment where possible.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global standardization program lowers central cost but forces high-growth markets into processes that slow customer response. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Distinguish where standardization creates enterprise value from where local variation is economically justified, and govern exceptions using end-to-end outcomes.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manual approval was introduced when data quality was poor, but current systems now validate the same information automatically. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reassess the underlying risk and current controls, test the approval's incremental value, and remove or redesign it if automation now provides equivalent or stronger assurance.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An executive summary must reconcile optimistic customer research with weak economics and uncertain regulation. What should it emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The assumptions connecting demand, economics and regulatory feasibility, the strength and conflict of evidence, and which uncertainties could reverse the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposed strategy is supported by historical data, expert opinion and market momentum, but all three may reflect the same underlying assumption. What should you test?$qb$, $qb$Whether the apparently independent evidence is actually correlated around one assumption, and how the strategy performs if that assumption is wrong.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The same type of enterprise failure has appeared in different forms across several years despite repeated local fixes. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Look across events for recurring system structures, incentives, information delays and governance patterns, then intervene at the mechanism that keeps regenerating the failures.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave the success criteria and subsequent decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You face an irreversible strategic decision with potentially enormous upside, limited evidence and a meaningful chance of severe loss. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Identify what can still be learned before irreversibility, bound catastrophic downside, value delay and option structures, and make residual uncertainty and risk ownership explicit.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make the need for change undeniable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Each business unit is meeting its financial targets, yet enterprise cash flow and customer value are deteriorating because units shift costs and risks to one another. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign performance management around enterprise economics and cross-unit effects, with shared measures and decision rights that prevent local success from destroying total value.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with additional safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A governance process exists because of a catastrophic event twenty years ago, but the business, technology and controls have completely changed. What is the strongest reassessment?$qb$, $qb$Reconstruct the unacceptable outcome and causal mechanism, evaluate current exposure and controls from fundamentals, and retain only governance that still adds meaningful protection.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive extensive and conflicting intelligence before a strategic decision: models, expert opinions, customer signals and geopolitical analysis. What should your synthesis produce?$qb$, $qb$A decision-focused view of the few assumptions and signals that matter, evidence quality and dependence, credible counterarguments, and what could change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave the success criteria and subsequent decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
