  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Every function meets utilization targets, yet enterprise projects repeatedly wait for scarce expertise. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$How utilization targets, queues and shared-resource allocation interact to reduce flow, and whether local efficiency is creating enterprise delay.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic option has low current probability but would protect the company from a catastrophic industry shift. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess the option's carrying cost, uniqueness, downside protection and alternative ways to recreate it, then fund it proportionate to the exposure it hedges.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Local businesses optimize their own inventory and cash, causing shortages in shared production and distribution. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Use enterprise working-capital and service measures, align decision rights across the network, and remove incentives that reward local optimization at system cost.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A governance framework requires the CEO to approve many decisions that were risky when the company was small but are now routine. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Reassess current risk and capability, delegate routine decisions with clear thresholds and controls, and reserve CEO authority for consequences that genuinely require it.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy paper combines precise financial forecasts with uncertain assumptions about customer behavior. What should the synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Make assumption uncertainty as visible as numerical outputs, show sensitivity and alternative behaviors, and prevent model precision from being mistaken for confidence.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic case cites many experts, but most experts built careers around the technology being recommended. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess incentive and identity bias, seek credible counter-expertise and empirical evidence, and distinguish genuine consensus from a community invested in one paradigm.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several businesses independently optimize schedules, yet shared infrastructure experiences severe peaks and idle periods. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Cross-business demand correlation, scheduling incentives and shared-capacity governance that local optimization fails to see.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic investment is unattractive on expected value but protects against a low-probability event that could threaten the enterprise. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess it as resilience or insurance: quantify catastrophic exposure, alternatives, carrying cost and risk tolerance rather than rejecting it solely on average return.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Business units improve their own service metrics by routing difficult cases to a central team that has no corresponding target protection. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Measure end-to-end outcomes and transferred workload, create shared accountability and redesign incentives so local performance cannot be improved by exporting complexity.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A governance structure has separate committees for risks that increasingly overlap. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Map current risk interactions and decision needs, consolidate where integrated judgment adds value, and preserve specialist challenge only where it contributes distinct expertise or independence.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic report contains strong quantitative evidence for one path but repeated qualitative warnings from experienced operators. What should synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Test whether the quantitative model omits mechanisms the operators observe, assess both evidence types critically, and show how the decision changes if the warnings are valid.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The enterprise repeatedly suffers failures at interfaces between individually high-performing businesses. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$The incentives, information gaps, decision rights, feedback delays and shared-resource dependencies at the interfaces rather than demanding still better local performance.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You face a strategic decision where both action and delay could create irreversible losses. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Map the irreversibilities on both sides, value information and option structures, identify minimum commitments that preserve future choices and make residual risk ownership explicit.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-030$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your strongest potential successor is ready, but their departure from a current role would create short-term performance risk elsewhere. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Optimize for enterprise succession, plan the transition and replacement, accept bounded short-term disruption and avoid using current performance dependence to block leadership renewal.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A governance system has grown through decades of risk events and is now too slow for the current business. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Start from today's unacceptable outcomes, obligations and decision needs, derive the minimum effective controls and authorities, and remove inherited layers that no longer add independent protection.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-036$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must advise the board amid conflicting models, political intelligence, customer behavior and expert judgment. What should your synthesis deliver?$qb$, $qb$A transparent map of decision-driving assumptions, source quality and incentives, evidence dependence, strongest counter-case, uncertainty and the signals that would change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-037$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leadership philosophy closely associated with you has become part of company identity, but future success may require abandoning much of it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate enduring purpose from your personal doctrine, openly update the philosophy based on evidence, legitimize alternatives and ensure the institution can evolve beyond its current leader.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor proposes a software tool claiming it will increase team productivity by 30%. The proposal includes glowing client testimonials. How do you evaluate this proposal?$qb$, $qb$Analyze raw data sources behind the productivity claim, assess compatibility with existing team workflows, and request a structured trial.$qb$, $qb$Present the proposal to your team lead, highlighting the positive client testimonials and potential efficiency gains.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for a discount on licensing fees before deciding whether to pilot the software tool.$qb$, $qb$Consult with another internal department to see if they have utilized similar vendor tools in the past.$qb$, $qb$Accept the vendor claims as valid industry benchmarks and recommend adopting the tool to department leadership.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customer satisfaction scores for your service queue drop by 10% over two months. Initial comments point to general delays. How do you identify the root cause?$qb$, $qb$Map ticket lifecycle stages, analyze response times across sub-categories, isolate specific bottleneck steps, and prototype targeted workflow fixes.$qb$, $qb$Host a brainstorming session with customer service agents to collect qualitative ideas on why response times are slowing.$qb$, $qb$Implement a daily ticket resolution quota for agents to raise overall processing speed across the board.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the score drop to department leadership, recommending temporary staffing additions during peak hours.$qb$, $qb$Send follow-up feedback surveys to dissatisfied customers to gather detailed individual complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$To speed up customer order dispatch, your team cuts inspection steps. While dispatch speed improves by 20%, return rates due to missing items rise by 15%. What is your reaction?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the systemic trade-off, restore critical quality checks, and redesign dispatch staging to improve speed without compromising accuracy.$qb$, $qb$Add an extra warning label to shipping boxes advising customers to check contents upon arrival.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the faster dispatch speed while creating a dedicated fast-track return queue to handle increased returns.$qb$, $qb$Revert strictly to the original slower dispatch workflow to eliminate order errors completely.$qb$, $qb$Pass return rate metrics to the customer service team to manage post-delivery complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A weekly reconciliation process requires 8 hours of manual copy-pasting across three legacy spreadsheets because 'it has always been done this way.' How do you rebuild it?$qb$, $qb$Deconstruct the underlying data inputs and final reporting requirements, remove redundant copy-paste steps, and design an automated data flow.$qb$, $qb$Search online for spreadsheet macro templates to speed up the existing copy-pasting steps.$qb$, $qb$Divide the 8 hours of manual copy-pasting among four team members so each person spends only 2 hours.$qb$, $qb$Suggest reducing reconciliation frequency from weekly to monthly to save team operational hours.$qb$, $qb$Continue executing the process as documented to ensure historical tracking continuity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-073$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor claims its service cut processing time by 25% for another company. Your team is considering a purchase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how the result was measured, the comparison baseline, the other company's context, implementation requirements, and evidence relevant to your own environment before drawing a conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for references from two more customers.$qb$, $qb$Assume the result is realistic because the vendor published it publicly.$qb$, $qb$Reject the claim because vendor case studies are marketing material.$qb$, $qb$Choose the vendor if the quoted price is lower than your current operating cost.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-074$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customer complaints about delayed updates have increased. Teams respond by sending more reminders, but complaints continue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map where updates are generated, delayed, or lost, identify the root point causing the communication gap, and test a fix at that point rather than adding more reminders.$qb$, $qb$Increase the frequency of reminders again.$qb$, $qb$Ask customer-facing staff to apologize more quickly when updates are late.$qb$, $qb$Create a new dashboard showing all overdue updates.$qb$, $qb$Escalate repeated complaints to department managers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-080$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new intake rule reduces errors entering your process, but completion time rises because customers submit more follow-up questions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the end-to-end effect, identify why the rule creates extra questions, and redesign it so error prevention does not simply shift effort downstream.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because intake accuracy improved.$qb$, $qb$Remove the rule because total completion time is worse.$qb$, $qb$Ask customer support to answer the extra questions faster.$qb$, $qb$Add more instructions without checking which part is confusing.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-081$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly review contains 18 checks added over many years. People spend hours completing them, but no one can explain which risks several checks still address. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the decisions and risks the review must support today, map each check to those needs, and remove or redesign controls that no longer add necessary assurance.$qb$, $qb$Ask team members which checks they think are unnecessary.$qb$, $qb$Automate all 18 checks so the review takes less time.$qb$, $qb$Remove the checks that have not found an issue in the last year.$qb$, $qb$Keep the review unchanged because every check was originally added for a reason.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-119$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows sales conversion improved after a new script was introduced. At the same time, the team received fewer but better-qualified leads. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the effect of lead quality from the script by examining comparable cohorts or other evidence before attributing the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Credit the new script because conversion increased after it was introduced.$qb$, $qb$Reject the script because the lead mix changed.$qb$, $qb$Ask salespeople whether they think the script helped and use the majority view.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher conversion without discussing possible causes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-120$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An approval queue is repeatedly delayed. Everyone assumes the approvers are too slow, but the delays vary widely by request type. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze queue stages and request types to locate where waiting actually occurs, then address the specific cause rather than assuming approver speed is the problem.$qb$, $qb$Ask approvers to respond faster.$qb$, $qb$Add more approvers to the queue.$qb$, $qb$Escalate every request older than two days.$qb$, $qb$Increase the official turnaround time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-126$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team automates a step and saves four hours each week, but another team now spends six extra hours resolving exceptions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the total process impact, understand why exceptions increased, and redesign the automation or handoff so the net system improves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the automation because your team's productivity target improved.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to automate its exception handling.$qb$, $qb$Remove the automation immediately.$qb$, $qb$Transfer ownership of the exceptions to a third team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-127$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report is produced every week because leadership requested it years ago. Current leaders rarely reference it, but preparing it takes several hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the decision or control the report is supposed to support today, verify whether that need still exists, and redesign or stop the report accordingly.$qb$, $qb$Ask recipients whether they still read the report.$qb$, $qb$Automate the report so preparation takes less time.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the report to half its current length.$qb$, $qb$Continue it because leadership originally requested it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-163$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says customer complaints rose after a process change and therefore the process change caused the increase. The timing matches, but you have not seen the underlying data. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the complaint trend, definitions, affected groups, other changes in the same period, and plausible alternative causes before concluding causation.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the complaint data and compare the period before and after the process change.$qb$, $qb$Accept the link provisionally because the timing is strong evidence, but monitor another month.$qb$, $qb$Ask several experienced colleagues whether they also think the process change is the cause.$qb$, $qb$Recommend reversing the process immediately because customer complaints are too important to risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-164$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team misses a weekly cutoff by a few minutes almost every week. The first suggestion is to add more reminders. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Trace where time is actually lost across the workflow, identify the recurring constraint, and test a change aimed at that cause.$qb$, $qb$Review the last few misses, identify the most likely cause, and try a focused improvement.$qb$, $qb$Add automated reminders because they are quick to implement and may improve timeliness.$qb$, $qb$Tell the slowest contributor to submit earlier so the rest of the process has more time.$qb$, $qb$Move the cutoff later so the team can meet it consistently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-170$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team speeds up its processing time by skipping a review step, but another team now spends more time correcting the resulting errors. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the end-to-end effect on total effort and turnaround time, then redesign the workflow so local speed does not create greater downstream cost.$qb$, $qb$Restore the review step and monitor whether total turnaround time improves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster process but ask the downstream team to improve how it handles corrections.$qb$, $qb$Track only the errors that cause major rework and ignore small corrections.$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because your team's processing target has improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-171$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A task is said to require three days because it has always been completed through the same sequence of steps. You are asked whether the time can be reduced safely. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Break the task into required outcomes, real constraints, dependencies, and assumptions, then rebuild the process from what is actually necessary.$qb$, $qb$Identify the slowest current steps and look for ways to shorten each of them.$qb$, $qb$Copy a faster process used by another team and adapt it to your work.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the time allowed for every existing step by the same percentage.$qb$, $qb$Accept three days as the proven requirement because repeated experience is strong evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-209$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A presentation claims that one branch performs better because its employees are more experienced. The chart shows correlation but no analysis of other differences. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the underlying branch data, experience distribution, workload, customer mix, and other plausible factors before accepting experience as the cause of better performance.$qb$, $qb$Compare average employee experience between the high- and low-performing branches.$qb$, $qb$Accept experience as the likely explanation but state that the chart does not prove causation.$qb$, $qb$Ask branch managers whether experienced employees generally perform better.$qb$, $qb$Use the chart to recommend hiring more experienced employees at other branches.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-210$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team keeps adding people to a manual checking process, yet errors continue at roughly the same rate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze where errors enter the process and why they survive the checks, then test a change aimed at the failure point instead of adding more checkers.$qb$, $qb$Review recent errors and give the checking team additional instructions.$qb$, $qb$Add another checker because more review should eventually reduce errors.$qb$, $qb$Make the existing checkers accountable for every error that escapes.$qb$, $qb$Accept the error rate because additional checking has already been tried.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-211$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer wants an answer today, but one technical test that could change your recommendation will finish tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what is known, how the pending test could change the recommendation, give a conditional answer if useful, and commit to a final update when the test finishes.$qb$, $qb$Give your current best recommendation and mention that one test is still pending.$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow and give no recommendation today.$qb$, $qb$Give a firm answer based on current evidence because the customer asked for a decision today.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior technical colleague to choose the answer so you are not responsible for the uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-216$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A faster customer intake process increases the number of incomplete cases reaching the processing team. Intake metrics improve while total resolution time worsens. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full journey from intake to resolution, identify which missing information creates downstream rework, and redesign intake so speed and completeness are balanced.$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster intake and give processors a checklist for handling incomplete cases.$qb$, $qb$Restore the old intake process immediately.$qb$, $qb$Add staff to the processing team to absorb the extra incomplete cases.$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because the intake team's performance metric has improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-217$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A rule requires every request to receive two approvals, even though many requests are routine and low value. You are asked whether the process can be improved safely. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the risk each approval is meant to control, determine which request characteristics actually create that risk, and test a proportionate approval path for lower-risk requests.$qb$, $qb$Remove one approval from all requests and monitor whether problems increase.$qb$, $qb$Keep two approvals but ask approvers to respond faster.$qb$, $qb$Copy a one-approval process used by another department.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because two approvals are inherently safer than one.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-255$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a training program is successful because employees who attended have higher performance scores than employees who did not. Attendance was voluntary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether attendees differed before training, how performance was measured, and what other factors could explain the difference before attributing the higher scores to the program.$qb$, $qb$Compare the average scores of attendees and non-attendees more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the program probably helped but avoid claiming it was the only cause.$qb$, $qb$Ask attendees whether they believe the training improved their performance.$qb$, $qb$Recommend expanding the program because the performance difference is already visible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-256$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service team keeps missing a response target. Adding reminders and daily follow-ups has not improved the result. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the work from request arrival to response, identify where requests wait or loop, and test a change aimed at the actual bottleneck.$qb$, $qb$Increase the number of reminders and make them more visible.$qb$, $qb$Ask the slowest team members to explain why they miss the target.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the response target temporarily so the team can rebuild confidence.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the target may be unrealistic because repeated follow-up has not worked.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-257$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A backup supplier can reserve capacity for you today for a small fee. Your main supplier may still deliver on time, but will not confirm until next week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the reservation cost with the impact of having no backup, assess the main supplier's risk, and reserve proportionate backup capacity if the downside justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Pay the reservation fee immediately because backup capacity is always safer.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the main supplier's confirmation so you do not spend money unnecessarily.$qb$, $qb$Switch the full order to the backup supplier now.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether paying for unused backup capacity would be acceptable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-262$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces call-handling time by transferring difficult cases quickly to a specialist group. The first team's metric improves, but the specialist queue grows sharply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the total customer journey and case mix, identify which cases truly need specialist expertise, and redesign routing so local speed does not create a larger downstream queue.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist group to increase its staffing during peak periods.$qb$, $qb$Set a limit on how many cases the first team may transfer each day.$qb$, $qb$Restore the old call-handling target so agents spend longer on every case.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because customers reach specialists faster when a case is difficult.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-263$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team produces a daily report because leaders once needed same-day visibility. Most leaders now review the information weekly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconfirm the decision need the report was created to serve, identify any remaining daily-use cases, and redesign the frequency and content around current requirements.$qb$, $qb$Change the report to weekly because most leaders now read it weekly.$qb$, $qb$Keep producing it daily but automate more of the preparation.$qb$, $qb$Ask leaders to confirm individually whether they still read the daily report.$qb$, $qb$Continue daily reporting because reducing information frequency could create risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-301$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team claims a new script has reduced customer call time because average call duration fell this month. Call volume and customer mix also changed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare like-for-like calls, examine the volume and customer-mix changes, check other relevant measures, and only then judge whether the script contributed to the reduction.$qb$, $qb$Compare this month's average call time with the month before the script.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the script probably helped but state that other factors may also matter.$qb$, $qb$Ask agents whether the script feels faster to use.$qb$, $qb$Roll the script out more widely because the headline metric improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-302$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly submit expense claims with the same missing information even though the form marks the fields as required. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe how claims are being completed, identify why the required fields are still missed, and test a form or workflow change that prevents or catches the omission at the right point.$qb$, $qb$Send a reminder explaining which fields are mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Reject every incomplete claim and ask employees to resubmit.$qb$, $qb$Add more required-field labels to the form.$qb$, $qb$Accept that some employees will always make mistakes and keep correcting claims manually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-308$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A quality team reduces defects by adding more checks, but delivery time grows and teams begin bypassing the checks on urgent work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine where defects originate, which checks add real control, and why urgent work bypasses them, then redesign the system to control risk without creating an unusable path.$qb$, $qb$Keep all checks and enforce them more strictly on urgent work.$qb$, $qb$Remove the newest checks until delivery time improves.$qb$, $qb$Create a separate fast path for urgent work with no checks.$qb$, $qb$Accept longer delivery because quality is more important than speed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-309$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A weekly approval meeting exists because approvals once required everyone to be physically present. The team now works digitally. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what the approval actually needs—evidence, decision rights, and record—then determine whether those requirements still need a weekly meeting or can be met more directly.$qb$, $qb$Move the weekly approval meeting online and keep the same agenda.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the meeting from one hour to thirty minutes.$qb$, $qb$Replace the meeting with email approvals without reviewing the control requirements.$qb$, $qb$Keep the meeting because approvals are important and synchronous discussion is safer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-310$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Three teams report different numbers for the same KPI because they use different cut-off times. You need one figure for an executive update today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the definitions and cut-off times, choose the figure that matches the executive reporting rule, and explain any material difference rather than blending incomparable values.$qb$, $qb$Use the most recently updated figure.$qb$, $qb$Average the three numbers to avoid favoring one team's data.$qb$, $qb$Use the figure from the team that owns the largest part of the process.$qb$, $qb$Choose the number closest to last week's trend so the update remains consistent.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-347$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague argues that a new sales approach works because two large deals closed after the team started using it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine the deal pipeline before the change, how the approach was used, other factors affecting the deals, and a broader set of outcomes before concluding the approach caused the wins.$qb$, $qb$Review the two successful deals in detail to identify what the new approach contributed.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the approach probably helped but avoid claiming it guarantees success.$qb$, $qb$Ask the salespeople involved whether they believe the approach made the difference.$qb$, $qb$Recommend the approach to every team because the two deals demonstrate strong potential.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-348$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring approval is often delayed because requests arrive with missing attachments. Staff already receive a checklist before submitting. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate why attachments are still missed, then test a submission control or workflow cue that detects the missing item before the request enters approval.$qb$, $qb$Send the checklist again whenever a request is incomplete.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to the checklist about each attachment.$qb$, $qb$Reject incomplete requests without explanation so submitters learn the requirement.$qb$, $qb$Add another approver to catch missing attachments earlier.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-354$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team introduces a strict response-time target. Staff begin closing requests quickly and reopening them later when the issue is not fully solved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how the target is shaping behavior, measure end-to-end resolution and reopen rates, and redesign the measure so speed is rewarded only alongside durable resolution.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff not to close requests until they are fully solved while keeping the same target.$qb$, $qb$Increase monitoring of reopened requests and discipline repeat offenders.$qb$, $qb$Remove the response-time target completely.$qb$, $qb$Keep the target because fast initial response remains valuable to customers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-355$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team requires a manager signature on every routine update because paper forms once needed physical authorization. Updates are now digital and fully logged. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what assurance the signature was meant to provide, determine whether the digital audit trail and decision rights already satisfy it, and redesign approval only if the control objective remains protected.$qb$, $qb$Replace the signature with an email approval from the manager.$qb$, $qb$Remove manager approval from every update because the system is digital.$qb$, $qb$Keep the signature requirement but use electronic signatures.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process unchanged because manager authorization is an important control.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-356$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks whether service quality improved. Customer satisfaction rose, complaint volume fell, but repeat-contact rates increased. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what each measure captures, investigate whether repeat contacts signal unresolved issues, and present a balanced conclusion rather than treating the favorable metrics as sufficient.$qb$, $qb$Say quality improved because two of the three measures moved positively.$qb$, $qb$Focus on repeat contacts because they contradict the other measures.$qb$, $qb$Average the percentage changes into one overall quality score.$qb$, $qb$Use customer satisfaction as the main measure because it comes directly from customers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-393$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A survey result says 90% of respondents support a proposed change, but only 10% of invited employees responded. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine who responded, whether the sample differs from non-respondents, the question wording, and other evidence before treating the 90% as representative support.$qb$, $qb$Report the 90% result but clearly mention the 10% response rate.$qb$, $qb$Assume support is strong because respondents had the opportunity to oppose the change.$qb$, $qb$Run the same survey again and hope more employees respond.$qb$, $qb$Use the result to proceed because 90% is a decisive majority.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-394$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A shared mailbox receives repeated questions that are answered in an automated reply, but people continue sending follow-up emails. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the follow-up questions to see what the automated reply fails to resolve, then test changes to the message or process that address the actual remaining need.$qb$, $qb$Make the automated reply longer and include more information.$qb$, $qb$Send the automated reply a second time when people follow up.$qb$, $qb$Tell users that their question was already answered automatically.$qb$, $qb$Assign more staff to handle the follow-up emails.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-395$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A machine shows an unusual warning but is still operating normally. Stopping it now delays production; continuing could worsen a fault if the warning is serious. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the warning's known severity and available diagnostics, assess the consequence of failure versus a controlled stop, and choose a proportionate action with a clear trigger for shutdown.$qb$, $qb$Stop the machine immediately because equipment warnings should never be ignored.$qb$, $qb$Continue production until performance actually changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the most experienced operator whether they would keep running it.$qb$, $qb$Reset the warning and continue if it does not reappear immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-400$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A company rewards teams for reducing their own costs. One team saves money by transferring work to another department, increasing total company cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure total end-to-end cost and redesign the incentive or allocation rule so local savings count only when they do not create larger downstream cost.$qb$, $qb$Ask the receiving department to become more efficient at the transferred work.$qb$, $qb$Reverse the transfer and tell the first team to find savings elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Stop rewarding cost reduction altogether.$qb$, $qb$Keep the incentive because each manager should control only their own budget.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-401$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team requires every presentation to contain twenty standard slides because the template was designed years ago for executive reviews. Most current audiences need only half of them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the information each current audience actually needs and which slides satisfy mandatory requirements, then redesign the template around those needs rather than the historical slide count.$qb$, $qb$Allow presenters to hide slides they think are unnecessary.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the standard template from twenty slides to ten for everyone.$qb$, $qb$Keep all twenty slides but move less useful ones to an appendix.$qb$, $qb$Keep the template because standardization is more important than presentation length.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-439$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team says a new reminder system reduced late submissions because lateness fell after it was introduced. The submission deadline was also moved by two days. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the effects of the deadline change and reminder system using comparable periods or groups before concluding which change reduced lateness.$qb$, $qb$Compare lateness before and after the reminder system and note the deadline change as a limitation.$qb$, $qb$Assume both changes contributed and keep them both.$qb$, $qb$Ask submitters which change helped them more.$qb$, $qb$Credit the reminder system because it was specifically designed to reduce lateness.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-440$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team receives many incomplete internal requests even though every field on the request form is mandatory. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review which information is incomplete and how users are entering it, then test validation, examples, or workflow changes that improve information quality rather than merely field completion.$qb$, $qb$Add more mandatory fields so users provide more detail.$qb$, $qb$Reject incomplete requests and tell users to try again.$qb$, $qb$Send a detailed guide explaining what good answers look like.$qb$, $qb$Assign someone to correct incomplete requests before they reach the team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-441$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may double an order next month but will not confirm for two weeks. Reserving production capacity now has a cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the probability and value of the larger order, compare reservation cost with the risk of lost capacity, and reserve a proportionate option if the expected value supports it.$qb$, $qb$Reserve all required capacity now because losing the order would be worse.$qb$, $qb$Wait for confirmation so no reservation cost is wasted.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to pay the reservation cost before you consider holding capacity.$qb$, $qb$Ask production to keep the capacity informally without recording a commitment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-446$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A company shortens approval time by allowing more people to approve requests. Approval is faster, but inconsistent decisions increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine decision criteria, training, authority and feedback across approvers, then redesign the system so added capacity does not reduce consistency.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the number of approvers back to the original group.$qb$, $qb$Keep all approvers but require managers to review inconsistent decisions afterward.$qb$, $qb$Publish more examples and assume consistency will improve.$qb$, $qb$Accept some inconsistency because faster approval is the main objective.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-447$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A weekly status call exists mainly so everyone can read updates that are already written in a shared tool. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what value the live call should add beyond written status, keep only discussion or decisions that need interaction, and move routine reading to asynchronous updates.$qb$, $qb$Shorten the call and ask people to read updates faster.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the call immediately because the information is already written.$qb$, $qb$Keep the call but stop requiring written updates.$qb$, $qb$Keep both because repetition reduces the chance that someone misses information.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-485$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a team is more productive because it completed more tasks this month. The average task size was smaller than last month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare workload size, complexity, quality and available capacity before concluding that the higher task count represents higher productivity.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher task count but mention that tasks were smaller.$qb$, $qb$Divide task count by team size and use that as productivity.$qb$, $qb$Accept that productivity improved because more work items were completed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team whether they felt more productive this month.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-486$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A helpdesk receives many password-reset requests even though users have a self-service reset tool. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze why users bypass or fail with self-service, observe the failure points, and test changes to access, guidance or the reset flow before adding helpdesk capacity.$qb$, $qb$Send users more reminders about the self-service tool.$qb$, $qb$Tell helpdesk staff to redirect every caller to self-service.$qb$, $qb$Add a chatbot that repeats the reset instructions.$qb$, $qb$Accept the requests because some users prefer human help.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-487$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delivery route may be disrupted by a planned public event, but the final road closures will be announced only tomorrow. Your delivery is early the next morning. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify credible alternative routes now, assess their cost and timing, and set a trigger based on the closure announcement so the delivery can switch without last-minute confusion.$qb$, $qb$Choose the alternative route now to remove all risk.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final closure announcement before doing any planning.$qb$, $qb$Keep the normal route because closures are not yet confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the driver to decide on the morning based on traffic conditions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-492$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A company introduces a bonus for rapid issue closure. Closure time improves, but customer complaints about repeat problems increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how the bonus is shaping behavior, include durable resolution and repeat-issue measures, and redesign the incentive so fast closure is valuable only when the problem stays solved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the bonus but tell staff not to close issues prematurely.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the bonus amount so staff feel less pressure.$qb$, $qb$Remove the bonus and return to the old system.$qb$, $qb$Accept more repeat issues because customers still receive faster initial closure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-493$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team asks every customer for the same ten pieces of information because the form was designed for the most complex case. Most cases need only four. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which information is necessary for each decision or risk level, then redesign the form so common cases ask only what is needed and complex cases trigger additional questions.$qb$, $qb$Remove the six least-used questions for everyone.$qb$, $qb$Keep all ten questions because standard forms are easier to manage.$qb$, $qb$Create two separate forms, one short and one long, and let customers choose.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers to leave irrelevant fields blank.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-531$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report says complaints fell by 30%, but the company also changed how complaints are categorized during the same period. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the old and new definitions, recalculate or bridge the data where possible, and avoid attributing the full change to service improvement until measurement effects are separated.$qb$, $qb$Report the 30% fall but mention that categorization changed.$qb$, $qb$Assume complaints genuinely fell because the new categories apply to everyone.$qb$, $qb$Ask customer-facing staff whether they feel complaints have decreased.$qb$, $qb$Use only data collected after the categorization change and ignore the earlier period.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-532$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees often submit duplicate requests because they cannot tell whether their first request was received. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the visibility gap, test an acknowledgement and status mechanism, and measure whether duplicate submissions fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees not to submit a second request within 24 hours.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to delete duplicate requests when they see them.$qb$, $qb$Add a warning on the form saying duplicates slow processing.$qb$, $qb$Increase capacity so duplicate requests do not create delays.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-533$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a one-time chance to buy extra material at a low price. Future demand is uncertain and the material expires after six months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate credible demand ranges, expiry and holding cost, compare them with the saving and shortage risk, and buy only the quantity justified across those scenarios.$qb$, $qb$Buy the maximum amount because the price may not be available again.$qb$, $qb$Buy nothing because uncertain demand makes extra inventory risky.$qb$, $qb$Split the difference and buy half the offered quantity.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier what quantity other customers are buying and follow that benchmark.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-538$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its on-time completion rate by starting work earlier, but this causes upstream teams to rush inputs and make more errors. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full flow and error impact, identify the real timing constraint, and redesign planning so one team's punctuality does not depend on pushing instability upstream.$qb$, $qb$Keep the earlier start and ask upstream teams to improve their accuracy.$qb$, $qb$Return to the old start time immediately.$qb$, $qb$Add a review step to catch upstream errors after work starts.$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because on-time completion is the team's main target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-539$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer request requires three internal handoffs because different departments historically owned different pieces. Technology now allows one team to complete all three steps. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which ownership, control or expertise requirements still justify separate handoffs, then redesign the flow around the minimum necessary boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Move all three steps to one team immediately because technology now permits it.$qb$, $qb$Keep the handoffs but automate the transfer between teams.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the three handoffs to two as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Keep the structure because departmental ownership should not change solely due to technology.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-577$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a process change improved quality because error counts fell. The total number of transactions also fell substantially. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare error rates as well as counts, examine transaction mix and other changes, and only then judge whether underlying quality improved.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower error count but mention lower volume.$qb$, $qb$Assume quality improved because fewer errors reached customers.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether the new process feels more accurate.$qb$, $qb$Use the error count because absolute customer errors are what matter most.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-578$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team spends significant time answering requests for information that is already available in a dashboard, but users say the dashboard is 'too complicated.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe the common information users seek, identify where the dashboard creates friction, and test a simpler view, navigation or explanation targeted to those needs.$qb$, $qb$Train users to use the existing dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Create a document containing the most requested numbers.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to stop answering questions already covered by the dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Add more dashboard features so users can find everything in one place.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-579$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier's quality score has declined slightly for two months, but no major failure has occurred. Switching suppliers would be costly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the trend and causes, increase proportionate monitoring or corrective action, and define thresholds that would trigger stronger intervention or switching.$qb$, $qb$Switch suppliers now before a major failure occurs.$qb$, $qb$Continue as normal because no major failure has happened.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier for reassurance that quality will improve.$qb$, $qb$Reduce orders by half immediately without discussing the trend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-584$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team speeds up processing by batching requests once per day. Their efficiency improves, but urgent requests now wait many hours before entering the batch. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure efficiency and delay across request types, keep batching where it adds value, and design an exception path or different cadence for genuinely time-sensitive work.$qb$, $qb$Stop batching and return to processing every request immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep batching and tell urgent requesters to submit earlier.$qb$, $qb$Add a second daily batch for all requests.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because team efficiency improved overall.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-585$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team stores printed copies of digital records because this was required before electronic records became legally accepted. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the current legal, audit and operational evidence requirements, then remove paper retention only where the digital record fully satisfies those underlying needs.$qb$, $qb$Stop printing immediately because digital records are now legally accepted.$qb$, $qb$Keep printing because paper provides an additional backup.$qb$, $qb$Print only the most important records.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether they prefer paper or digital storage.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-623$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports that employee satisfaction improved after introducing flexible hours. During the same period, several dissatisfied employees left. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine who remained and who left, response rates, comparable groups and other changes before concluding that flexible hours caused the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Report the satisfaction improvement but note that some employees left.$qb$, $qb$Assume flexible hours helped because the remaining employees are more satisfied.$qb$, $qb$Ask current employees whether flexible hours improved their experience.$qb$, $qb$Recommend expanding flexible hours because the survey moved positively.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-624$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team receives many requests marked 'urgent,' making it difficult to identify the few that genuinely require immediate action. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze why requesters use the urgent label, define clear urgency criteria and consequences, and test a triage method that distinguishes true time-critical work.$qb$, $qb$Tell requesters to use 'urgent' less often.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to review every urgent request manually as soon as it arrives.$qb$, $qb$Create a second label called 'very urgent.'$qb$, $qb$Treat all requests as normal priority because the urgent label has lost meaning.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-625$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier says a critical part may arrive one day late, but there is a costly local substitute available today. Production can absorb a one-day delay if nothing else slips. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the probability and downstream impact of the delay, define the latest safe point for buying the substitute, and preserve the option rather than purchasing or rejecting it immediately.$qb$, $qb$Buy the substitute now because the part is critical.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the supplier because production can absorb one day.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the original order and switch fully to the local substitute.$qb$, $qb$Ask production to decide because they will experience the delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-630$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces waiting time by keeping more work in progress at once. Individual tasks start sooner, but total completion time begins to increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure queue size, flow and completion time, identify whether excess work in progress is causing congestion, and set limits or sequencing rules that optimize total flow.$qb$, $qb$Keep starting work quickly because visible waiting time has improved.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to work faster on each open task.$qb$, $qb$Stop accepting new work until every current task is finished.$qb$, $qb$Add more status meetings to track all open work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-631$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team requires a weekly spreadsheet because a former system could not produce the information directly. The current system can now generate the same data automatically. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the spreadsheet provides any additional decision, control or transformation value, and retire it if the automated system output fully meets the underlying need.$qb$, $qb$Keep the spreadsheet but populate it automatically from the system.$qb$, $qb$Stop the spreadsheet immediately because automation is available.$qb$, $qb$Continue it as a backup in case the system report fails.$qb$, $qb$Ask users whether they prefer the spreadsheet or system report.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-669$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows that one team has fewer errors than another, but the teams handle different types of work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare error rates within comparable work types, complexity and volume before concluding that one team performs better.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower-error team as the better performer but note that work differs.$qb$, $qb$Compare total errors because customers experience errors regardless of work type.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers which team's work is harder.$qb$, $qb$Use the dashboard ranking because the metric was designed for comparison.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-670$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly miss a small but important step even though it appears in the middle of a long procedure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe when and why the step is missed, then test a point-of-action cue, workflow control, or procedure redesign that makes the critical step hard to overlook.$qb$, $qb$Highlight the step in bold in the procedure.$qb$, $qb$Send a reminder every month about the missed step.$qb$, $qb$Discipline employees who miss it more than once.$qb$, $qb$Add another person to check whether the step was completed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-671$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delivery partner warns that fuel disruption may affect tomorrow's route. An alternate partner can be booked now but charges a cancellation fee if unused. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the disruption likelihood and delivery impact, compare the cancellation fee with failure cost, and reserve the alternate if the option value justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Book the alternate immediately because the delivery must be protected.$qb$, $qb$Wait until disruption is confirmed to avoid cancellation cost.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the original partner and switch fully to the alternate.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer whether they will pay the cancellation fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-676$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces customer waiting by moving unfinished cases to another queue at the end of each day. The first queue looks better, but total unresolved cases rise. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure cases across the full lifecycle, remove incentives to shift unfinished work between queues, and redesign ownership and metrics around actual resolution.$qb$, $qb$Ask the second queue to increase capacity.$qb$, $qb$Stop all transfers between queues immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep the transfer but report both queue sizes together.$qb$, $qb$Accept the process because customers leave the first queue faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-677$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly report includes several charts because they were requested by a leader who left two years ago. Current users rarely discuss them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which decisions each chart is meant to support today, remove or redesign charts with no current purpose, and retain any required historical or control information separately.$qb$, $qb$Remove every chart that has not been discussed recently.$qb$, $qb$Keep all charts because historical continuity is valuable.$qb$, $qb$Move unused charts to an appendix but continue producing them.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to vote on which charts they like.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-715$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report shows that customers who use a certain feature stay longer. A colleague concludes the feature causes retention. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether longer-term customers are simply more likely to discover the feature, compare similar customer groups and timing, and test alternative explanations before claiming causation.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the feature probably improves retention but describe the evidence as correlational.$qb$, $qb$Compare retention of feature users and non-users in more detail.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers who use the feature whether it makes them stay.$qb$, $qb$Recommend promoting the feature because the retention association is strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-716$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team repeatedly misses one field when transferring information from an email into a system. Training has already been repeated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe the transfer step, identify why that field is easy to miss, and test a structured input, automation, or point-of-entry control that removes reliance on memory.$qb$, $qb$Run another training session focused on the missed field.$qb$, $qb$Add the field to a checklist beside every workstation.$qb$, $qb$Discipline repeat errors after the next reminder.$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to review every transferred record.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-717$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A warehouse may need extra temporary space next month. Booking now secures a good rate, but final demand will not be known for two weeks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate demand ranges and overflow impact, compare reservation or cancellation cost, and secure a proportionate option if losing space would be more costly.$qb$, $qb$Book all the extra space now because rates may rise.$qb$, $qb$Wait for final demand so no unused space is paid for.$qb$, $qb$Book half the estimated space as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the warehouse provider what they recommend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-722$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reduces overtime by postponing unfinished work to the next month. Monthly cost looks better, but backlog steadily grows. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure backlog, service impact and total workload alongside overtime, and redesign capacity or prioritization so cost improvement does not come from shifting work across reporting periods.$qb$, $qb$Keep the overtime reduction and add a backlog target.$qb$, $qb$Allow more overtime until the backlog is cleared.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff to finish more work during normal hours.$qb$, $qb$Accept the backlog because reducing overtime was the department goal.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-723$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires customers to sign two separate forms containing almost identical declarations because two departments historically created their own forms. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the legal and operational purpose of each declaration, determine whether one combined declaration can satisfy both, and redesign only after confirming the underlying requirements.$qb$, $qb$Combine the forms immediately because the wording is almost identical.$qb$, $qb$Keep both forms because each department owns one.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers to sign only the first form and copy the signature to the second.$qb$, $qb$Digitize both forms without changing their content.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-761$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says customer satisfaction is improving because the latest survey score is higher. The latest survey had far fewer responses than usual. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check response count, respondent mix, confidence or variability, and comparable prior surveys before concluding the higher score represents a real improvement.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher score but note the smaller sample.$qb$, $qb$Assume the score improved because respondents had no reason to answer differently.$qb$, $qb$Combine the latest score with the previous score to smooth the result.$qb$, $qb$Use the latest score because it is the most current evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-762$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team keeps receiving requests with the wrong category selected, sending work to the wrong queue. Users say the category names are confusing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze which categories are confused, test clearer labels or guided questions, and measure whether routing errors fall before adding more manual correction.$qb$, $qb$Send users definitions of every category.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to reclassify requests after they arrive.$qb$, $qb$Add more categories so choices are more precise.$qb$, $qb$Tell users to read the guidance before selecting a category.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-763$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A maintenance contractor may not be available on your preferred date next month. Reserving a backup now costs a small non-refundable fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess how critical the maintenance date is, the probability and impact of losing the primary contractor, and reserve backup capacity if the option cost is justified.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the backup immediately because the fee is small.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the primary contractor confirms.$qb$, $qb$Book only the backup and cancel the primary request.$qb$, $qb$Ask the primary contractor to guarantee availability.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-768$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department improves utilization by scheduling staff at nearly full capacity. Small unexpected tasks now cause frequent delays across the week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure variability and delay, recognize the cost of having no capacity buffer, and redesign scheduling with appropriate slack for unpredictable work.$qb$, $qb$Keep full utilization and ask staff to work faster when unexpected tasks arrive.$qb$, $qb$Add overtime whenever delays occur.$qb$, $qb$Reduce planned work by the same amount every week without analyzing demand.$qb$, $qb$Ignore small delays because utilization is the more important metric.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-769$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team asks for three signatures on a routine document because each manager historically wanted visibility. The digital system already records who reviewed and accepted it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what authority or assurance each signature was meant to provide, compare that need with the digital record, and remove redundant signatures only where the underlying control is already satisfied.$qb$, $qb$Remove all signatures because the system records reviews.$qb$, $qb$Keep all signatures because managers still need visibility.$qb$, $qb$Reduce three signatures to one as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Replace handwritten signatures with electronic signatures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-807$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports that a new workflow increased output by 20%, but staffing also increased by 25%. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare output per unit of capacity, quality and workload mix before concluding that the workflow itself improved productivity.$qb$, $qb$Report the 20% output increase and note the staffing increase.$qb$, $qb$Say productivity declined because staffing grew faster than output.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether the new workflow feels faster.$qb$, $qb$Keep the workflow because total output is higher.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-808$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers frequently choose the wrong option in an online form even though the options are accurately described. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe how customers interpret the choices, identify the confusing distinction, and test clearer labels, examples or guided questions at the decision point.$qb$, $qb$Add more detailed descriptions below each option.$qb$, $qb$Tell support staff to correct the choice after submission.$qb$, $qb$Send customers a guide before they use the form.$qb$, $qb$Add a confirmation box saying they selected the correct option.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-809$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A shipment may arrive late, and you can pay a modest fee now to reserve weekend unloading capacity. If the shipment is on time, the reservation will be unused. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the lateness probability and cost of losing another day, compare it with the reservation fee, and reserve capacity if preserving the option has sufficient value.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the weekend capacity because the fee is modest.$qb$, $qb$Do not reserve because the shipment may arrive on time.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the shipment is confirmed late even if capacity may be gone.$qb$, $qb$Ask the carrier to pay the reservation fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-814$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves first-response time by sending immediate standard replies, but customers increasingly contact the team again because the replies do not solve their questions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure repeat contact and total resolution time, distinguish where standard replies help, and redesign the response metric and process around useful resolution rather than first-touch speed alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the standard replies and train customers to provide clearer questions.$qb$, $qb$Remove all standard replies.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to every standard reply.$qb$, $qb$Accept repeat contact because first-response performance has improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-815$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team creates a separate monthly summary from a dashboard because leaders once could not access the dashboard. Leaders now have full access and use it regularly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Determine whether the summary adds interpretation or decisions beyond the dashboard, and retire or redesign it if its original access purpose no longer exists.$qb$, $qb$Stop the summary immediately because leaders can see the dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Keep the summary because leaders may still appreciate convenience.$qb$, $qb$Automate the summary but keep the same content.$qb$, $qb$Ask leaders whether they like receiving it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-853$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a campaign worked because sales rose during the campaign week. A major competitor was also out of stock that week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine comparable periods, customer sources and the competitor effect before attributing the sales increase to the campaign.$qb$, $qb$Report the sales increase but mention the competitor shortage.$qb$, $qb$Assume both factors helped and call the campaign successful.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether they noticed the campaign.$qb$, $qb$Repeat the campaign because sales clearly improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-854$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team receives frequent follow-up calls asking whether submitted forms were received. The forms usually arrive correctly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the confirmation gap and test an automatic receipt or visible status so users know the form arrived without calling.$qb$, $qb$Tell users to wait 24 hours before calling.$qb$, $qb$Give the team a standard response for follow-up calls.$qb$, $qb$Publish the normal processing time more prominently.$qb$, $qb$Add staff to answer confirmation calls faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-855$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier warns that a material price may rise next month. Buying early saves money if the rise occurs but increases inventory if it does not. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate credible price and demand scenarios, compare potential saving with holding and obsolescence cost, and choose a proportionate early purchase if expected value supports it.$qb$, $qb$Buy the maximum now before the price rises.$qb$, $qb$Wait because the increase is not certain.$qb$, $qb$Buy half the expected need as a simple compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier whether they personally expect the price to rise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-860$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces backlog by closing old requests that have had no customer response. Customers later reopen many of them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure reopen patterns and customer contact timing, distinguish genuinely abandoned requests from unresolved ones, and redesign closure rules around durable resolution rather than backlog appearance.$qb$, $qb$Keep closing old requests and handle reopened ones as new work.$qb$, $qb$Stop closing any request without explicit customer confirmation.$qb$, $qb$Send more reminders before closure.$qb$, $qb$Accept reopenings because the backlog metric improves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-861$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department requires managers to attend every routine review because years ago only managers could access the underlying data. Access is now available to the whole team. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what decision or authority still requires a manager, and remove mandatory attendance where data access was the only reason while retaining manager involvement for genuine decisions.$qb$, $qb$Stop manager attendance immediately because everyone has data access.$qb$, $qb$Keep managers because their presence improves discipline.$qb$, $qb$Let managers decide individually whether to attend.$qb$, $qb$Replace managers with senior employees at every review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-901$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A venue for an important customer event has not confirmed availability. A suitable backup venue will hold the date until tomorrow for a refundable deposit. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess lateness probability and the cost of losing another day, compare it with the reservation fee, and reserve capacity if preserving the option has sufficient value.$qb$, $qb$Reserve because the fee is modest.$qb$, $qb$Do not reserve because it may arrive on time.$qb$, $qb$Wait until lateness is confirmed even if capacity may be gone.$qb$, $qb$Ask the carrier to pay the fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-945$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Reported errors fell after a new checklist, but the definition of an error changed at the same time.$qb$, $qb$Reconcile old and new definitions and compare like-for-like evidence before crediting the checklist.$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Assume the checklist must be very effective.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff if it feels useful.$qb$, $qb$Keep it regardless of cause.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-946$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$New employees repeatedly abandon one onboarding step despite accurate written instructions.$qb$, $qb$Observe the step, collect user evidence, and test whether timing, design, access or context—not just wording—is causing abandonment.$qb$, $qb$Send the instructions again.$qb$, $qb$Add more text.$qb$, $qb$Have managers chase completion.$qb$, $qb$Remove the step.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-947$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A venue for a customer event is unconfirmed; a suitable backup can be held until tomorrow for a refundable deposit.$qb$, $qb$Hold the backup while confirming the primary venue, define a switch point, and release it if no longer needed.$qb$, $qb$Move to the backup now.$qb$, $qb$Wait and risk losing it.$qb$, $qb$Book both permanently.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-953$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees still print and sign an acknowledgement even though secure digital acceptance records identity and timestamp.$qb$, $qb$Confirm the legal and control purpose of the signature and retire paper only if digital acceptance fully satisfies it.$qb$, $qb$Stop printing immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep paper forever as backup.$qb$, $qb$Print only important acknowledgements.$qb$, $qb$Let employees choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-954$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Active customers rate a service highly, yet weekly usage has dropped sharply.$qb$, $qb$Explain that satisfaction covers remaining users, investigate who stopped using the service, and present both signals rather than using satisfaction as the whole answer.$qb$, $qb$Say customers like it.$qb$, $qb$Say customers dislike it.$qb$, $qb$Average the measures.$qb$, $qb$Wait for more data.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-991$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Trained employees score higher than others, but the strongest employees were selected for the training.$qb$, $qb$Use comparable groups or before-and-after evidence to account for selection before attributing higher scores to training.$qb$, $qb$Accept the result because trained employees score higher.$qb$, $qb$Assume selection and training contributed equally.$qb$, $qb$Ask participants whether training helped.$qb$, $qb$Expand the training immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-992$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A form gets technically complete submissions that still lack enough context for staff to act.$qb$, $qb$Identify the missing decision-relevant context, redesign prompts or examples, and test whether follow-up questions decrease.$qb$, $qb$Add more mandatory fields.$qb$, $qb$Call every submitter.$qb$, $qb$Send longer instructions.$qb$, $qb$Reject all vague submissions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-993$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier may miss delivery because of a transport strike; a secondary supplier can hold stock for 24 hours at no charge.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the secondary stock while verifying impact, define a switch trigger, and release it if the primary delivery is secured.$qb$, $qb$Switch immediately.$qb$, $qb$Do nothing because the strike may end.$qb$, $qb$Buy from both.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to decide.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-998$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service team improves response time by forwarding difficult cases immediately, overloading specialists.$qb$, $qb$Measure end-to-end resolution and transfer quality, clarify which cases truly need specialists, and redesign routing for total performance.$qb$, $qb$Add more specialists.$qb$, $qb$Stop all transfers.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because first response improved.$qb$, $qb$Set a transfer quota.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-999$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly approval committee exists because managers once lacked authority for small purchases; current policy now grants that authority.$qb$, $qb$Confirm which purchases still need collective oversight and remove committee review where current authority already satisfies the control.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the committee completely.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because group review is safer.$qb$, $qb$Let managers decide whether to attend.$qb$, $qb$Raise the purchase limit further.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1000$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Overall satisfaction is flat because one region improved strongly while another large region declined similarly.$qb$, $qb$Show the offsetting regional movements, quantify their contribution, and explain why the stable average hides materially different experiences.$qb$, $qb$Say satisfaction is unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the improving region.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the declining region.$qb$, $qb$Average the regional percentages again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2823$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report shows average customer spend increased, but many low-spend customers stopped buying during the same period. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine customer retention and spend distributions, separate mix change from increased spending by comparable customers, and avoid calling the average rise a pure growth improvement.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher average spend as success.$qb$, $qb$Say performance worsened because customers left.$qb$, $qb$Ignore customers who stopped buying because they are no longer active.$qb$, $qb$Compare only total revenue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2824$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees frequently select 'Other' on a request form even though the correct category usually exists. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze which requests go to 'Other,' observe why existing labels are not chosen, and test clearer categories or guided selection before adding more manual triage.$qb$, $qb$Remove the 'Other' option completely.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to read the category guide.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to recategorize every 'Other' request.$qb$, $qb$Add more categories immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2825$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may increase an order next month. Reserving raw material now costs a small holding fee, but waiting may mean the material is unavailable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the probability and impact of the larger order, compare the holding fee with shortage risk, and reserve a proportionate amount if the option value justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Reserve all material now.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the customer confirms.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to pay the holding fee.$qb$, $qb$Reserve half the possible amount without analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2828$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team meets its speed target by closing cases before downstream verification is finished. Reopened cases are rising. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure true end-to-end completion, change closure criteria and incentives to include required verification, and address the reopened backlog.$qb$, $qb$Keep the speed target and add a separate reopen target.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff not to close cases early.$qb$, $qb$Stop measuring speed entirely.$qb$, $qb$Add more downstream verifiers without changing closure rules.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2829$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires two managers to approve a low-risk request because years ago neither manager had full authority alone. Current policy now gives either one authority. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the current authority rule and remove redundant dual approval for cases it covers, retaining additional review only where another control requires it.$qb$, $qb$Keep both approvals because more control is safer.$qb$, $qb$Remove all approvals for low-risk requests.$qb$, $qb$Let employees choose whether to get one or two approvals.$qb$, $qb$Alternate which manager approves each week.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2830$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A business unit reports higher profit, but the increase came mainly from selling an asset rather than normal operations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the one-off asset gain from operating profit, quantify both, and explain the underlying recurring performance before drawing a trend conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher profit as improved performance.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the asset sale because it is legitimate income.$qb$, $qb$Exclude the asset sale entirely from all reporting.$qb$, $qb$Wait until next quarter before discussing profitability.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1129$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A sales team reports a higher win rate this quarter, but it also stopped recording very early-stage opportunities as formal opportunities. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile the definition change, compare win rates on equivalent opportunity stages, and avoid treating the headline increase as pure sales improvement.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher win rate and mention the process change in a footnote.$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement because only serious opportunities should count.$qb$, $qb$Compare total wins instead of win rate.$qb$, $qb$Ask salespeople whether they feel more effective.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1130$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team receives many 'urgent' requests late in the day because requesters know urgent items are handled first. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze which requests are genuinely time-critical, define objective urgency criteria and service expectations, and redesign triage so the label cannot be used simply to jump the queue.$qb$, $qb$Handle all urgent requests first because requesters chose that label.$qb$, $qb$Tell people not to mark requests urgent after 3 p.m.$qb$, $qb$Remove the urgent category entirely.$qb$, $qb$Ask a manager to approve every urgent request.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1131$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may require weekend support for a launch. Reserving specialist cover now has a fee, but waiting could leave no one available. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the likelihood and business impact of weekend need, compare it with the reservation fee, and secure proportionate cover if preserving availability is worth the cost.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the full team immediately.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the customer confirms.$qb$, $qb$Ask specialists to remain informally available without booking them.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer weekend support cannot be guaranteed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1136$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A call center reduces call length by asking customers to call back for issues needing research. Average handling time improves, but repeat calls rise. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure total customer contacts and resolution time, examine the incentive created by the handling-time target, and redesign the process and metrics around durable resolution.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because shorter calls improve capacity.$qb$, $qb$Stop measuring handling time entirely.$qb$, $qb$Tell agents not to ask customers to call back unless absolutely necessary.$qb$, $qb$Add more agents to handle the repeat calls.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1137$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team requires a manager signature on a report because years ago only managers could access the source system. Analysts now have controlled access and are accountable for the report. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify whether the manager signature still provides a distinct approval or control; remove it if access was the only original reason and current accountability is sufficient.$qb$, $qb$Remove the signature immediately because analysts have access.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because manager signatures increase confidence.$qb$, $qb$Replace the manager signature with a peer signature.$qb$, $qb$Let each analyst decide whether a signature is needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1138$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customer revenue is stable, but one large customer increased spending while many smaller customers reduced spending. A leader asks whether the customer base is healthy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the stable total alongside concentration and segment movements, explain the dependency on the large customer, and assess the broader customer trend separately.$qb$, $qb$Say the customer base is healthy because revenue is stable.$qb$, $qb$Say it is unhealthy because smaller customers declined.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the large customer's growth.$qb$, $qb$Average spending changes across all customers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1175$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer-service team reports fewer complaints after a new process, but the company also made the complaint form harder to find. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check complaint access and alternative signals, compare like-for-like periods, and avoid concluding service improved until reduced reporting is separated from reduced dissatisfaction.$qb$, $qb$Report fewer complaints as improvement.$qb$, $qb$Assume customers would find the form if the issue were serious.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff whether customers seem happier.$qb$, $qb$Keep the new process because complaint counts are lower.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1176$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly submit incomplete travel requests because one required cost is usually unknown when the form must be started. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine the timing conflict, redesign the form or workflow to allow a controlled estimate or later completion of that field, and test whether incomplete requests fall.$qb$, $qb$Make the cost field mandatory so requests cannot be incomplete.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to wait until every cost is known before starting.$qb$, $qb$Ask approvers to chase missing costs.$qb$, $qb$Remove the cost field entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1177$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may postpone a launch. You can cancel a specialist booking today for free, but after today there is a fee; keeping the booking protects the original date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the probability and impact of postponement versus losing specialist availability, and decide whether the option value justifies keeping the booking past the free-cancellation point.$qb$, $qb$Cancel now because it is still free.$qb$, $qb$Keep the booking because the original date is still official.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to remain available without a booking.$qb$, $qb$Let the customer decide whether you should pay the future fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1182$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A warehouse team meets its picking-speed target by leaving packing checks until the end of the shift, creating a late bottleneck. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full order flow, identify how the speed target shifts work downstream, and redesign sequencing and metrics around completed orders rather than picking alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the picking target and add overtime for packing.$qb$, $qb$Tell pickers to help packing after they finish.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the picking target without analyzing flow.$qb$, $qb$Accept the bottleneck because picking performance is strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1183$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A form requires a supervisor signature because the old system could not identify who submitted it. The new authenticated system records submitter identity automatically. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the signature serves any other approval purpose and remove it if identity verification was the only reason.$qb$, $qb$Remove it immediately because the system records identity.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because signatures show accountability.$qb$, $qb$Replace it with a peer signature.$qb$, $qb$Let each supervisor choose whether to sign.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1184$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report shows total support cost fell, but the number of customers served also fell significantly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare cost per customer, service level and workload mix before concluding that support became more efficient.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower total cost as efficiency improvement.$qb$, $qb$Say efficiency worsened because fewer customers were served.$qb$, $qb$Ask support staff whether work feels easier.$qb$, $qb$Use only total cost because that is what the budget measures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1221$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A branch reports much faster service after moving complex customers to appointments on later days.$qb$, $qb$Measure total time to resolution for all customers, including deferred appointments, before concluding service genuinely became faster.$qb$, $qb$Accept the faster same-day service metric.$qb$, $qb$Reject appointments because they delay complex customers.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether they prefer appointments.$qb$, $qb$Compare only walk-in waiting time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1222$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Users frequently enter placeholder text in a mandatory comments box because they have nothing meaningful to add.$qb$, $qb$Determine what information the field is meant to capture, make it conditional or redesign the prompt if it is not always needed, and measure whether useful comments improve.$qb$, $qb$Tell users not to enter placeholders.$qb$, $qb$Increase the minimum character count.$qb$, $qb$Remove the field immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask reviewers to reject placeholder comments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1223$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key subcontractor may be unavailable for a planned weekend activity. Another qualified firm can reserve a crew for a modest cancellation fee.$qb$, $qb$Assess the probability and impact of unavailability, compare it with the reservation fee, and hold backup capacity if the option is economically justified.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the backup automatically.$qb$, $qb$Wait for confirmation from the subcontractor.$qb$, $qb$Book both firms for the work.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to pay for the backup.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1228$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A factory improves machine utilization by delaying routine maintenance until machines are idle, but breakdowns are increasing.$qb$, $qb$Measure total output, downtime and maintenance effects, and redesign scheduling so high utilization does not come at the cost of reliability.$qb$, $qb$Keep utilization high and repair breakdowns faster.$qb$, $qb$Return to the old maintenance schedule without analysis.$qb$, $qb$Add more maintenance staff.$qb$, $qb$Ignore breakdowns if production targets are met.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1229$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A paper form requires a supervisor stamp because the old filing room used stamps to identify approved forms. The digital system now records approval identity and time.$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the stamp serves any remaining legal or control purpose, and remove it if digital approval fully satisfies the underlying requirement.$qb$, $qb$Remove the stamp immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because physical stamps look official.$qb$, $qb$Replace it with a scanned stamp.$qb$, $qb$Let supervisors choose whether to stamp.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1230$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product's total sales grew, but growth came entirely from heavy discounts while units sold barely changed.$qb$, $qb$Separate unit demand, price and discount effects, and explain whether the sales growth reflects stronger underlying demand or reduced price realization.$qb$, $qb$Report sales growth as strong performance.$qb$, $qb$Say performance declined because discounts increased.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on units sold.$qb$, $qb$Wait until discounts end before analyzing performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1267$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says productivity improved because completed tasks increased, but the team also began counting several small subtasks separately. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile the measurement definition and compare equivalent units of work before concluding that underlying productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the increase because more items were completed.$qb$, $qb$Use hours worked instead of task counts.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether they feel more productive.$qb$, $qb$Keep the new measure because it shows more detail.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1268$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers often select the wrong delivery option because the cheapest option appears first even when it cannot meet their requested date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use customer date requirements to guide or filter valid choices, clarify the trade-off at selection, and test whether wrong selections and later corrections fall.$qb$, $qb$Move the fastest option to the top for everyone.$qb$, $qb$Add more text describing every delivery option.$qb$, $qb$Let support correct the option after ordering.$qb$, $qb$Remove the cheapest option entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1269$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A venue for a training event is confirmed, but there is a moderate chance of transport disruption that day. A nearby backup can be held until next week at low cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess disruption likelihood and attendee impact, compare it with the hold cost, and preserve the backup if the option value is worthwhile until the risk becomes clearer.$qb$, $qb$Switch to the backup now.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the risk because the main venue is confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Book both venues fully.$qb$, $qb$Ask attendees to decide which venue should be used.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1274$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its response SLA by sending quick acknowledgements, but actual resolution time is unchanged and customer satisfaction falls. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate acknowledgement from resolution, review the metric incentive, and redesign service measures and workflow around meaningful progress and final resolution.$qb$, $qb$Keep the SLA because fast acknowledgement is valuable.$qb$, $qb$Stop acknowledgements completely.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers that resolution naturally takes longer.$qb$, $qb$Add a satisfaction target without changing the workflow.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1275$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires employees to print a system-generated report and sign it because the old system could not retain reviewer identity. The current system does. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the paper signature serves any additional requirement and retire it if the system's authenticated review record fully satisfies the control.$qb$, $qb$Stop printing immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep printing because paper is easier to audit.$qb$, $qb$Replace the signature with initials typed into the report.$qb$, $qb$Let each reviewer decide whether to print.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1276$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports that average project margin improved, but several low-margin projects were cancelled before completion and excluded from the average. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how cancelled projects are treated, compare a consistent project population, and distinguish genuine margin improvement from selection effects.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher average margin as success.$qb$, $qb$Include every cancelled project at zero margin.$qb$, $qb$Ignore cancellations because they were not completed projects.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance whether the average looks reasonable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1313$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports that absence fell after a new attendance policy, but employees also began using more annual leave for short illnesses. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine total time away and how absence categories changed before concluding that the policy improved attendance.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower sickness absence as success.$qb$, $qb$Say the policy failed because annual leave increased.$qb$, $qb$Ignore annual leave because it is a different category.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers whether employees seem healthier.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1314$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers frequently abandon a form when asked for an account number they may not have available, even though the number can be found internally. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the account number is genuinely needed from the customer, test an internal lookup or alternate identifier, and measure whether abandonment falls.$qb$, $qb$Make the account-number field optional without checking the downstream need.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers where to find the number.$qb$, $qb$Ask support to complete abandoned forms for customers.$qb$, $qb$Keep the field because account numbers improve accuracy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1315$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier may be unable to deliver on the preferred day. A later delivery is acceptable, but reserving an alternative carrier now would protect the preferred date for a fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the value of the preferred date with the reservation fee and probability of supplier delay, and reserve the carrier only if preserving that date is worth the cost.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the carrier because the preferred date should be protected.$qb$, $qb$Do nothing because a later date is acceptable.$qb$, $qb$Switch permanently to the alternative carrier.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to pay the reservation fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1320$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its on-time metric by changing promised dates shortly before they are due. Customers still receive work at the same actual time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure performance against the original customer commitment and analyze date changes, then redesign the metric and controls so schedule performance cannot improve through rebaselining alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric because revised dates are official dates.$qb$, $qb$Stop allowing any date changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask teams to change dates earlier.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the issue if customers are not complaining.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1321$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly review includes a manual sign-off because the old database did not prevent incomplete records. The new system blocks submission until all required fields are complete. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the sign-off provides any additional judgment or control and remove it if completeness checking was its only purpose.$qb$, $qb$Remove the sign-off immediately because the system blocks blanks.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because human sign-off is always stronger.$qb$, $qb$Replace it with an automated signature.$qb$, $qb$Let each reviewer choose whether to sign.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1322$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product reports higher average order value, but the minimum order size was increased during the same period. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the mechanical effect of the new minimum from changes in customer purchasing behavior before claiming that customers are spending more voluntarily.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher average order value as growth.$qb$, $qb$Say the change is entirely due to the minimum order.$qb$, $qb$Compare only total revenue.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether they noticed the minimum change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1359$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A branch reports fewer customer cancellations after it stopped counting customers who never completed onboarding.$qb$, $qb$Reconcile the population definition and compare equivalent cohorts before concluding retention improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the lower cancellation rate.$qb$, $qb$Say retention worsened because incomplete onboarding is still loss.$qb$, $qb$Use total customer count instead.$qb$, $qb$Ask the branch whether customers seem happier.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1360$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly choose an incorrect expense category because two category names sound almost identical.$qb$, $qb$Analyze the confused choices, test clearer labels or guided selection, and measure whether recoding falls.$qb$, $qb$Send employees a longer category guide.$qb$, $qb$Add another reviewer.$qb$, $qb$Remove one category regardless of its purpose.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to be more careful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1361$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may need extra capacity next month; reserving it now costs little, but unused capacity cannot be refunded.$qb$, $qb$Estimate likely demand and shortage impact, compare them with the reservation cost, and reserve a proportionate amount if justified.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the maximum capacity.$qb$, $qb$Wait for certainty.$qb$, $qb$Reserve half as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to guarantee payment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1366$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces processing time by skipping early checks and correcting errors at the end; total rework rises.$qb$, $qb$Measure end-to-end effort and error flow, restore or redesign the checks where they prevent more downstream work than they cost.$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster early process.$qb$, $qb$Restore every old check without analysis.$qb$, $qb$Add more people to final correction.$qb$, $qb$Ignore rework if deadlines are met.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1367$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires two people to enter the same confirmation because the old system could not show who entered it. The new system records user identity.$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the second entry provides any other independent check and remove it if identity duplication was its only purpose.$qb$, $qb$Remove it immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because two entries are safer.$qb$, $qb$Replace the second entry with a manager email.$qb$, $qb$Let teams decide locally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1368$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report shows higher average revenue per customer after many small customers moved to a lower-priced self-service product outside the report.$qb$, $qb$Reconcile the customer population and product shift before concluding that spending among the overall customer base increased.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher average as growth.$qb$, $qb$Ignore self-service customers because they are on another product.$qb$, $qb$Say revenue quality worsened automatically.$qb$, $qb$Compare only total revenue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1405$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team says customer response improved because average email reply time fell, but it also began excluding weekends from the calculation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recalculate or compare results using a consistent time definition before attributing the improvement to changed performance.$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement because business-hour response is the more relevant measure.$qb$, $qb$Say performance did not improve because weekends were excluded.$qb$, $qb$Use customer satisfaction instead of response time.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff whether they reply faster now.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1406$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees frequently enter estimated completion dates that they later change because the form forces a date before enough information is known. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine when a reliable date becomes knowable, allow an appropriate 'not yet estimated' state or confidence range, and measure whether misleading date changes fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to make better estimates.$qb$, $qb$Require manager approval for every changed date.$qb$, $qb$Remove completion dates entirely.$qb$, $qb$Use a standard default date for uncertain cases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1407$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may bring a launch forward by one week. Reserving specialist support for both dates costs a modest extra fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the likelihood and impact of the earlier launch, compare the extra reservation cost with losing support, and preserve both dates if the option value warrants it.$qb$, $qb$Reserve both dates automatically.$qb$, $qb$Keep only the current official date.$qb$, $qb$Ask specialists to remain informally free for the earlier date.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer an earlier launch is impossible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1412$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its completion metric by splitting unfinished cases into several smaller cases and closing the easy parts. Customer issues remain unresolved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure resolution at the customer-issue level, examine the incentive created by the metric, and redesign completion rules so splitting work cannot create false success.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric because the smaller cases are genuinely completed.$qb$, $qb$Ban splitting cases under any circumstances.$qb$, $qb$Add a separate customer-satisfaction metric but keep the process.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to use fewer subcases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1413$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires a printed cover sheet because the old archive used it to identify document type. The digital repository now requires and validates document type metadata. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the cover sheet serves no other legal or operational purpose, then remove it if validated metadata fully replaces its identification function.$qb$, $qb$Stop printing it immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because auditors may prefer paper cues.$qb$, $qb$Upload a scanned blank cover sheet.$qb$, $qb$Let each team choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1414$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service reports lower cost per case, but the calculation now excludes cases transferred to another department. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Include or account for transferred cases and downstream cost consistently before concluding that end-to-end cost efficiency improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the lower cost because the team no longer handles transferred cases.$qb$, $qb$Say cost increased because another department now does work.$qb$, $qb$Use only the team's direct budget.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance whether the number is acceptable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1451$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says service quality improved because complaints fell, but customer volume also fell sharply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare complaints relative to customer volume and other service signals before concluding that quality improved.$qb$, $qb$Report fewer complaints as improvement.$qb$, $qb$Say quality worsened because volume fell.$qb$, $qb$Ignore volume because complaints are the official metric.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff whether service feels better.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1452$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Users frequently submit a request under the wrong service because two choices use similar internal terminology. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Study which two services users confuse, rename or guide the choice around user language, and measure whether misrouting falls.$qb$, $qb$Add more internal terminology to the descriptions.$qb$, $qb$Tell users to read the service catalogue.$qb$, $qb$Remove one service choice.$qb$, $qb$Have staff recategorize every wrong request.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1453$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may need a specialist one week earlier than planned; holding both dates has a modest fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the chance and impact of the earlier need, compare it with the hold fee, and reserve both dates only if the flexibility is worth it.$qb$, $qb$Hold both dates automatically.$qb$, $qb$Keep only the current date.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to stay informally free.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer an earlier date cannot be supported.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1458$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team meets its closure target by closing cases when work leaves their queue, even though another team still must finish them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure completion at the end-to-end customer outcome, not queue exit, and change the closure rule so work cannot be counted complete while downstream action remains.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because the team's work is finished.$qb$, $qb$Ban all transfers to other teams.$qb$, $qb$Add a second metric but leave closure unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Ask downstream teams to close faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1459$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A legacy approval step exists because the old system could not enforce a spending limit; the new system blocks transactions above the limit. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the approval adds any control beyond the new enforced spending limit and remove it if the system now fully performs its original function.$qb$, $qb$Remove approval immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because human approval is safer.$qb$, $qb$Replace it with an email confirmation.$qb$, $qb$Let each team decide whether to approve.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1460$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Average delivery time improved after very complex orders were moved into a separate reporting category. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recombine or normalize the reporting categories and compare equivalent orders before concluding that delivery performance improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement because the official category changed.$qb$, $qb$Ignore complex orders because they are now separate.$qb$, $qb$Compare only routine orders.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether deliveries feel faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1497$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports fewer overdue tasks after it changed the rule so tasks are overdue only five days after the original due date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare overdue performance using the same threshold and explain the rule change before claiming improvement.$qb$, $qb$Accept the lower overdue count because the new rule is official.$qb$, $qb$Say performance worsened because the threshold is longer.$qb$, $qb$Use total task count instead.$qb$, $qb$Ask team members whether deadlines feel easier to meet.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1498$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers repeatedly call after submitting an online request because the confirmation page does not show what happens next. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the uncertainty driving calls and test a confirmation that states receipt, next step and expected timing, then measure whether follow-up calls fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers not to call until the service deadline passes.$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to answer confirmation calls.$qb$, $qb$Send a longer instruction guide before submission.$qb$, $qb$Remove the confirmation page.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1499$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may need an extra room for an event. Holding it until next week has a small fee; booking later may be impossible. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the likelihood and impact of needing the room, compare that with the hold fee, and preserve the option if the expected value supports it.$qb$, $qb$Hold the room automatically because availability is uncertain.$qb$, $qb$Do not hold it because the need is unconfirmed.$qb$, $qb$Book the room fully now.$qb$, $qb$Ask the venue to keep it informally without a fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1504$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its 'first-time completion' metric by returning difficult requests to customers for minor clarifications instead of working them through. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine the return behavior and customer effort, define completion around genuine resolution, and adjust the metric so avoidable returns do not create better performance.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric because returned requests are incomplete inputs.$qb$, $qb$Ban all requests for clarification.$qb$, $qb$Add a customer-effort survey but keep the workflow.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to return fewer cases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1505$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report requires a manager initials box because the old spreadsheet could be edited after review. The new controlled system locks approved versions and records the reviewer. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the initials do not serve another requirement and remove them if version locking and reviewer identity fully provide the intended control.$qb$, $qb$Remove the initials immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep them because visible initials reassure users.$qb$, $qb$Add electronic initials as well as the system record.$qb$, $qb$Let managers choose whether to initial.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1506$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports higher output per employee after outsourcing a portion of routine work, but outsourced effort is excluded from the calculation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Include or account for outsourced capacity and comparable workload before concluding that total productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the higher output per employee because internal staff are more productive.$qb$, $qb$Say productivity declined because work was outsourced.$qb$, $qb$Compare only payroll cost.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether they feel more productive.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1543$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports faster case resolution after it stopped including cases waiting for third-party information in its average. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile the definition and show comparable end-to-end resolution, including or separately explaining third-party waiting time, before claiming improvement.$qb$, $qb$Accept the faster average because the team cannot control third parties.$qb$, $qb$Say performance worsened because excluded cases are difficult.$qb$, $qb$Use only active handling time.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether service feels faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1544$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers frequently enter a generic email address because a form requires a contact even when no individual contact is known yet. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Determine when a named contact is genuinely needed, allow a controlled pending state or alternative contact where appropriate, and measure whether false entries fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers not to use generic addresses.$qb$, $qb$Make the email field stricter.$qb$, $qb$Remove the contact requirement entirely.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to correct generic addresses later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1545$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may postpone an event. Cancelling the venue today is free, but keeping it for another week costs a small fee and preserves the date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the likelihood and impact of postponement versus losing the current date, and pay the hold fee only if preserving that option is worth it.$qb$, $qb$Cancel now because it is free.$qb$, $qb$Keep the venue because the date is currently confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Book a second venue as well.$qb$, $qb$Ask the venue to hold it without charging.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1550$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its backlog metric by moving difficult items into a separate 'special review' queue that is not counted. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full unresolved workload, define when special review is legitimate, and change reporting so moving an item cannot make backlog disappear.$qb$, $qb$Keep the separate queue because those items are genuinely different.$qb$, $qb$Ban special review entirely.$qb$, $qb$Add a note explaining the excluded queue.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to move fewer items.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1551$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires a printed approval page because the old system could not show the exact approved version. The new system locks the version at approval. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the paper page serves no additional legal or operational need, then retire it if version locking and approval history fully meet the control purpose.$qb$, $qb$Stop printing immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep printing because paper proves approval.$qb$, $qb$Print only high-value cases.$qb$, $qb$Let approvers choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1552$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports lower cost per transaction after moving some complex work to a specialist team whose cost is reported separately. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Include the specialist cost and comparable transaction scope, or clearly separate the transfer effect, before concluding end-to-end cost efficiency improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the lower cost because the team's direct cost fell.$qb$, $qb$Say efficiency worsened because specialist cost is higher.$qb$, $qb$Compare only headcount.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance whether the team metric is technically correct.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1589$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports improved customer retention after it stopped counting customers whose contracts ended naturally. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare retention using a consistent definition and explain the treatment of natural expiries before attributing the improvement to better customer loyalty.$qb$, $qb$Accept the improved retention because expired contracts are not cancellations.$qb$, $qb$Count every expiry as a lost customer.$qb$, $qb$Use only renewal revenue.$qb$, $qb$Ask account managers whether relationships feel stronger.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1590$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Users often select 'not applicable' on a form because answering one question requires information they receive only later in the process. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review when the information becomes available, move or condition the question appropriately, and measure whether false 'not applicable' responses fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell users not to select 'not applicable' unless it is truly irrelevant.$qb$, $qb$Make the question mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Remove the question entirely.$qb$, $qb$Ask reviewers to correct answers later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1591$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may need extra testing capacity for one week. Holding the capacity costs a small fee, while booking later could take two weeks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the likelihood and consequence of needing it, compare that with the hold fee, and reserve capacity if preserving the option is worthwhile.$qb$, $qb$Reserve it automatically because later booking is slow.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the need is certain.$qb$, $qb$Book the capacity fully now.$qb$, $qb$Ask the provider to keep it free informally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1596$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team meets its turnaround target by pausing the clock whenever it sends a question to another internal department, even for routine information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure end-to-end turnaround, define legitimate pause conditions, and change the metric so internal handoffs cannot hide customer waiting time.$qb$, $qb$Keep the pause because the team cannot control other departments.$qb$, $qb$Ban all clock pauses.$qb$, $qb$Ask other departments to answer faster.$qb$, $qb$Add a separate internal-response metric without changing turnaround.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1597$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires a manager to initial each page because the old paper pack could become separated. The new digital record is one locked document. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm page initials serve no other requirement and remove them if document integrity is now assured by the locked digital record.$qb$, $qb$Remove initials immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep them because managers are used to the control.$qb$, $qb$Add digital initials to every page.$qb$, $qb$Let managers decide case by case.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1598$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports higher revenue per employee after moving administrative work to a shared-services team whose headcount is excluded. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Account for shared-services effort and comparable work scope before concluding that overall labor productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the higher revenue per employee because department headcount fell.$qb$, $qb$Say productivity worsened because work moved elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Compare only salary cost.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance whether the calculation follows reporting rules.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1635$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Reported customer retention improved after natural contract expiries stopped being counted. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare retention using a consistent definition and explain the treatment of expiries before claiming improved loyalty.$qb$, $qb$Accept the new retention rate.$qb$, $qb$Count every expiry as a loss.$qb$, $qb$Use renewal revenue only.$qb$, $qb$Ask account managers whether relationships feel stronger.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1636$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$New starters often choose a placeholder response in an onboarding form because a requested identifier is not issued until their second week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move or condition the question to the point where the information is available, then measure whether false 'not applicable' responses fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell users not to select it incorrectly.$qb$, $qb$Make the question mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Remove the question.$qb$, $qb$Ask reviewers to correct answers later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1637$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer is considering adding a second workshop. A suitable facilitator can be tentatively reserved for a small fee, but later availability is uncertain. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate likelihood and consequence, compare them with the hold fee, and reserve capacity if preserving the option is worthwhile.$qb$, $qb$Reserve automatically.$qb$, $qb$Wait for certainty.$qb$, $qb$Book it fully now.$qb$, $qb$Ask the provider to hold it for free.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1642$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service desk improves its own turnaround by stopping the clock whenever it asks another internal team for information, while customers continue waiting. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure end-to-end turnaround, define legitimate pause conditions, and prevent internal handoffs from hiding customer waiting time.$qb$, $qb$Keep the pause because the team cannot control others.$qb$, $qb$Ban all pauses.$qb$, $qb$Ask other departments to answer faster.$qb$, $qb$Add another metric without changing turnaround.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1643$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supervisor still signs every page of a digital case pack because paper packs once became separated; the current system stores one locked approved file. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the initials serve no other requirement and remove them if document integrity is fully assured digitally.$qb$, $qb$Remove them immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep them because managers expect them.$qb$, $qb$Add digital initials to every page.$qb$, $qb$Let managers choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1644$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A business unit reports stronger revenue per employee after transferring support work to a central team whose effort is not included in the ratio. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Account for shared-services effort and comparable work scope before concluding overall labor productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the higher ratio.$qb$, $qb$Say productivity worsened because work moved.$qb$, $qb$Compare only salary cost.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance whether the ratio follows rules.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2143$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may need extra translation support during launch week; a specialist can be held for a modest cancellation fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate likelihood and consequence, compare with the fee, and reserve the specialist if preserving the option is worthwhile.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the resource immediately without evaluating alternative support options or likelihood.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to pay the cancellation fee and wait until the need is completely certain before booking.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to hold the resource for free until the exact requirement is confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the potential support need and handle any shortages as an emergency during launch.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
